WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:17.289
Tim Ferriss: Hello, ladies and germs, boys
and girls.

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:18.760
This is Tim Ferriss, obviously.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.680
Look at the shiny dome I have here.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.740
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.109
This is a rare, in-person filmed edition,
so thank you for joining us.

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:29.430
My guest today is Steven Pressfield.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.029
Steven was 52 years old before his first novel
was published.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:33.960
So you're saying there's a chance.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.070
This guy might have a chance.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:40.260
Since then, he has written the million-seller,
Gates of Fire and The War of Art, one of the

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:46.390
best titles of any book of all time, as well
as The Legend of Bagger Vance, A Man at Arms,

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.390
and many others.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.190
His newest book, the memoir Govt Cheese, and
I have it right here, this one is about those

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.440
years before the first publication.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.000
You can find him online.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.030
Steven, that's with a V, stevenpressfield.com.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.760
On Twitter @SPressfield and Instagram, @steven_pressfield.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.600
Steven, so nice to see you.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:05.960
Steven Pressfield: It's great to see you,
Tim.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.009
We've never met in person.

00:01:07.009 --> 00:01:08.009
Tim Ferriss: I know.

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:11.710
Steven Pressfield: We've had a lot of interaction,
so it's great to be here in the flesh.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:12.760
Thanks for having me.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.810
Tim Ferriss: Of course.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.800
I'm so happy we were able to do this.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:23.480
We were chatting and chatting and chatting,
not just remotely, not just in the last podcast,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.909
which I loved, which spurred me on to do all
sorts of experiments and fiction.

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:29.100
So thank you for that.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.149
But we were chatting and chatting and chatting
about creative process before hitting record

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:34.830
and both of us.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:35.950
Certainly, I was saying, "Oh…"

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:36.950
Steven Pressfield: We wasted half of it.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:38.509
Tim Ferriss: "...we just keep some of it."

00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:46.280
So I wanted to roll back and say thank you
again for really helping me and helping so

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:53.040
many people to pick up the pen, pick up the
keyboard, pick up the brush, whatever it might

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.790
be, and overcome this resistance.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.380
I'm sure we'll revisit resistance because
I don't want to assume that anyone listening

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.630
to this or watching this has heard our first
conversation.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.909
So why don't we set a little bit of context.

00:02:04.909 --> 00:02:10.360
In our first combo, we talked about the halfway
house that you lived in, in the cabin with

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.280
no electricity and a running water, et cetera.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.950
And my question that I thought we could start
with is about ambition.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:23.569
If I understand correctly, at the time you
felt guilty for having ambition.

00:02:23.569 --> 00:02:25.930
So I'd like you to do two things, if you wouldn't
mind.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:27.480
Just set the table a little bit.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.319
So how did you end up in a halfway house?

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:36.250
And then if you could talk about ambition
a little bit at that time, how it occurred

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:37.250
to you?

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:38.250
Steven Pressfield: Let's see if I can remember.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.290
This is in Govt Cheese.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:48.580
I was in the middle of a divorce, a middle
of a breakup, and living at my mother-in-law's

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:55.380
house in the country in North Carolina, had
a job delivering industrial food, institutional

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.380
food.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:06.700
I got fired from that job and just sort of
wound up in Durham, North Carolina with $20

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:13.500
in my pocket and found just a room in a place
that turned out to be a halfway house for

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:19.939
people coming out of mental institutions and
would be reintegrating into society.

00:03:19.939 --> 00:03:21.040
At that point, I actually got lucky.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.879
I found a job at a trucking company and so
I was settled into that place for a little

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:26.879
while.

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:34.680
But about ambition, I had a dream at that
time where — this is in the book The War

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:43.340
of Art, where I came back into my little basement
room and instead of it being a total mess,

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.819
the room had organized itself and my shirt
had all folded themselves and my boots had

00:03:48.819 --> 00:03:49.930
shined themselves and sat.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:55.870
Somehow I realized that the dream was about
ambition and the dream was sort of saying

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:03.659
to me, "You have ambition, Steve," which is
like you were just saying, Tim, at that time

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:10.970
my mindset was out of the '60s, the whole
thing of if you are ambitious individually,

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.739
it's kind of a betrayal of your brothers and
sisters.

00:04:14.739 --> 00:04:19.799
You really want to stand out above them or
achieve more than they, that kind of thing.

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:21.979
And so that was really a bad thing to do.

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:26.539
I really saw that as immoral, unethical, or
any of that kind of thing.

00:04:26.539 --> 00:04:28.250
And this dream kind of said the opposite.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.470
It said, "It's okay.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.910
It's okay for you to want to achieve something.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.390
It's okay for you to want to work hard at
something."

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:39.900
And that felt that as a real liberating kind
of moment for me.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.009
It took a long, long time for any of that
to pay off.

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:47.400
But that was sort of a turning point for me
where I said, "I do have ambition.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.310
I do want to achieve something and it's okay."

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:54.430
Tim Ferriss: And with that dream, when you
had that dream, when you wake up the next

00:04:54.430 --> 00:05:01.090
morning, I think for many people our dreams
come and go and some dreams stick.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:05.020
But oftentimes they come and go and the memory
degrades over time.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:11.270
Did you have that dream and then change things,
or at least your thinking or focus shortly

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:12.289
thereafter?

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:18.389
Or was it something that went away just as
most dreams do and then came back and revisited

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:21.020
you enough that you then started to change
your behavior?

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.289
What's your experience?

00:05:22.289 --> 00:05:26.490
Steven Pressfield: It absolutely stuck with
me, and you're absolutely right.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:30.039
At least in my experience, dreams are such
evanescent things.

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:32.639
I mean even from one minute to the next, they're
gone.

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:37.550
But this was like, I know you've read Jung's
Memories, Dreams, Reflections, right?

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:38.889
And it's his life stories, autobiography.

00:05:38.889 --> 00:05:44.300
One of the things he says in it at the start
is that he's not going to talk about events

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:50.139
in his life like meeting Mahatma Gandhi, but
he will talk about a dream he had when he

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:51.740
was six years old.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:52.810
And this was one of those for me.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:56.699
It was a big dream that never left me, that
always stuck with me.

00:05:56.699 --> 00:06:00.310
Took a long time for it to pay off, but it
stuck with me forever.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.620
Tim Ferriss: Now I recall, and I want you
to fact check this if I'm not getting this

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:11.680
right, but I remember that I believe you said
in the halfway house, these weren't, by and

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:12.729
large, stupid people.

00:06:12.729 --> 00:06:13.729
Steven Pressfield: No not all.

00:06:13.729 --> 00:06:14.729
Tim Ferriss: They seemed to be smart.

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:21.800
And perhaps their being in halfway house was
indicative of them not being able to cope

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:28.160
with the sort of collective delusion of normal
life in a sense.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.280
So my question, because I was listening to
a podcast recently, Hidden Brain, I'll give

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.600
it credit, and they were talking about discussing
literature that seems to support — I haven't

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:42.290
looked at the studies, so who knows that people
who would self-describe as depressed or who

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:48.509
are assessed as depressed have a more accurate
perception of reality in a number of different

00:06:48.509 --> 00:06:49.509
capacities.

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:55.720
So I'm wondering, how do you — and this
is a leading question, but how do you create

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.539
helpful delusions for yourself?

00:06:59.539 --> 00:07:04.910
Because even if it is maybe accurate to perceive
reality in a way that causes you to be depressed

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:11.110
or be in a halfway house, it's not necessarily
hugely functional, if that makes sense.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:14.270
And I say that as someone who's suffered from
a lot of depression.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:20.160
So this is a bit of meandering thinking out
loud question, but are there helpful delusions

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.050
that you can forge for yourself or cultivate
for yourself?

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:25.940
Steven Pressfield: I've never really even
thought about that, Tim, but I think it's

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:27.000
absolutely true.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.840
I mean, my version of that is denial.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:37.629
I believe that denial is the greatest thing
to, as a resource, to just simply — for

00:07:37.629 --> 00:07:41.752
instance, I'm a certain age and I'm in complete
denial of it.

00:07:41.752 --> 00:07:45.450
I absolutely refuse to accept it.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.150
And so that's a helpful delusion.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:52.220
I truly sort of work on that kind of thing.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:57.729
I see certain challenges for myself that if
challenges in the sense of if I let myself

00:07:57.729 --> 00:08:02.550
think in this certain manner, that's not going
to be a good thing.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.660
So I have to work at it.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:11.919
I'd be interested if you do the same thing,
Tim, where I will actively deny something

00:08:11.919 --> 00:08:15.600
like that or actively sort of brainwash myself.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.949
For instance, at the age I am now, it would
be very easy, which I don't even want to say

00:08:20.949 --> 00:08:26.780
what it is because I'm in denial yet is, it'd
be very easy to start thinking, "Okay, you

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:27.780
can take it easy now."

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:29.560
You've sort of hit a certain point.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:31.130
You can put it on cruise control.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:37.380
I know not if I do that, "I'm about one foot
on a banana peel, another one on a roller

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:38.380
skate."

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:44.790
So I definitely have to say to myself, "Okay,
I'm projecting 10 years into the future.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.510
What's my mindset going to be for that time?"

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:54.600
And I want it to be even more ambitious than
it was before.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.470
I'm not sure that's an answer to the question.

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:57.470
Tim Ferriss: No, it is.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:58.470
It is an answer.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:03.100
I think about this a lot in part because recently
on an extended hike, let's call it, multi-day

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.030
hike with a group of people.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:14.820
Two of them were very accomplished scientists
and scientific thinkers and two of them were

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:19.829
also very smart, but hyper, hyper optimistic
to the point where it almost to me seemed

00:09:19.829 --> 00:09:20.829
pollyanna-ish.

00:09:20.829 --> 00:09:27.720
However, they were also easily the two happiest
people in the group.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:30.510
And this stuck with me where I was like, "Okay,
sure.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:35.450
I might be able to see the grime under the
fingernails of life at every turn, but is

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.890
that actually serving me?"

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:39.540
Not convinced it always is.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.620
It helps for things like risk mitigation.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:48.450
But in the case of say, creative projects,
so we were chatting before we started recording

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:54.260
and I was mentioning that after our last conversation,
I ended up putting together my first fiction,

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:59.130
short story that I shared with the world,
which you were very kind to take a —

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.130
Steven Pressfield: Which was really good.

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:01.130
Tim Ferriss: — handful of time to look at.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:02.130
So thank you for that.

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:03.700
And your main feedback was like, "Don't overthink
it.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:05.310
Just keep going, keep going, keep going."

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.060
Which I did.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:12.130
And I've been working now on more fiction
because I'm enjoying the process of trying

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:14.500
to develop those muscles.

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:25.210
And when resistance shows up, when procrastination
shows up, it seems like having a set of beliefs,

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:32.079
whether they're true or not, to hold onto,
to white knuckle can be really helpful.

00:10:32.079 --> 00:10:35.420
Maybe I'll give you a real example because
I'll give you an example of resistance and

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:41.240
I'd be curious to know what you might do about
it because this is an area of your expertise,

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:42.980
or how you might think about it.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:44.930
I'll give you a very clear example.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:50.820
I've been editing a number of vignettes of
these greater houses I mentioned that exist

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.200
in this fantasy world
So there are these eight houses in this fictional

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:58.170
realm and when I started writing it was very
easy because I didn't have to connect any

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.170
dots.

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:03.060
And then it got increasingly more complex
as everything was interwoven.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:07.880
I took a note here of one of your characters
names.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:12.620
You're going to find this funny, I think,
and I'm looking for it, Arcadia.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:13.620
Arcadia.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.120
Steven Pressfield: Telamon of Arcadia?

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.380
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:17.420
Telamon of Arcadia.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:18.420
What a name.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:27.230
And I have found that I've been hemming and
hawing and going back and forth, and renaming,

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:29.570
and renaming, and renaming things like a hundred
times.

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:35.290
So this has become my way of not finishing
things.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.829
I suppose there are two questions.

00:11:36.829 --> 00:11:45.190
Number one, because there's one particular
piece which has to do with this greater house,

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:49.449
which is a house divided of spellcasters,
long story.

00:11:49.449 --> 00:11:54.240
And there are a lot of names in this one particular
piece, and I just let it go on and on and

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:55.240
on.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:58.250
It's my undone homework that's been sitting
there for now, probably two weeks.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:00.399
Even though I'm fiddling with it.

00:12:00.399 --> 00:12:05.440
So I suppose, first, or actually you can choose
which one you want to go with first, how do

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.730
you come up with your names of characters?

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:14.850
I would love to know how you just think about
naming fictional characters or fictional places.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:19.390
And then second, am I crazy?

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:23.089
Is this one of the stranger examples of how
resistance has come up?

00:12:23.089 --> 00:12:24.579
Steven Pressfield: I don't think you're crazy
at all.

00:12:24.579 --> 00:12:25.740
It's a really good question.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.470
I mean if you think about Game of Thrones,
the names of the characters there were so

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:31.470
fantastic.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:32.600
Tim Ferriss: So good.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:34.310
Steven Pressfield: Daenerys Targaryen.

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:35.310
Brienne of Tarth.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:36.889
What was her name, Brienne?

00:12:36.889 --> 00:12:39.810
I know Scott Fitzgerald used to keep a file.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.699
I'm sure a lot of writers do.

00:12:41.699 --> 00:12:46.151
Any time they'd come across a great name in
real life, they'd write it down and he had

00:12:46.151 --> 00:12:47.870
a whole list.

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:55.600
I do think in some crazy way, you've got to
get the name right and if you do get it right

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:58.220
then — it's like the character is a living
thing, right?

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.220
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:03.769
Steven Pressfield: They won't really speak
for you if you don't have them with the right

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:05.199
name.

00:13:05.199 --> 00:13:07.090
I don't think resistance at all.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:08.839
Tim Ferriss: Which is very appropriate for
spellcasters, the power of the name.

00:13:08.839 --> 00:13:10.339
Steven Pressfield: Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:10.339 --> 00:13:15.270
I mean, in real life, think of how parents
take so much time with the name they're going

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:19.079
to give their kid because they feel like,
"Gee, if I give them the wrong name, they're

00:13:19.079 --> 00:13:25.010
going to turn out to be — if I give them
the right name, Gwyneth Paltrow, something

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:26.190
like that.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.290
So I don't think that is resistance.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:33.930
I would say, don't worry about it, take your
time and let the names come.

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:34.930
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:35.930
It's good to hear.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:36.930
All right.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:38.399
Steven Pressfield: I myself spend a lot of
time in the names.

00:13:38.399 --> 00:13:41.440
Tim Ferriss: How do you grease the wheels
for that?

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:47.330
Steven Pressfield: You just put the word out
to the muse that you need some names.

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:52.410
And it seems to me like they sort of pop into
your head rather than sitting down —

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:53.410
Tim Ferriss: Thinking about it.

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:54.680
Steven Pressfield: — doing a list or something
like that.

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:55.680
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:56.680
All right.

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:59.779
Steven Pressfield: But one thing I do is anytime
something does pop into my head, I will write

00:13:59.779 --> 00:14:00.930
it down.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:05.480
Even if it's for another — it might be for
a book, three books down the line.

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.540
But names are really important, I think.

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:08.639
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:14:08.639 --> 00:14:09.750
So that makes me feel a lot better.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:13.000
I'm going to continue to plod along on this.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:14.000
And it's making progress.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.110
[BREAKING NEWS CLIP]
Tim Ferriss: Pardon the interruption, folks,

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:19.110
but I do have some news.

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:23.360
The fiction that Steve and I are talking about,
this thing I’ve been working on, is now

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:24.360
live.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:30.649
The first two short-fiction pieces are live
in audio form on something called the COCKPUNCH

00:14:30.649 --> 00:14:31.649
podcast.

00:14:31.649 --> 00:14:32.649
I’m not making that up.

00:14:32.649 --> 00:14:39.040
And each one is just around five minutes long,
very short, and you can find them at COCKPUNCH.com/podcast

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.040
or wherever you find your podcasts.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.399
Just look for COCKPUNCH and it’ll be there.

00:14:43.399 --> 00:14:49.410
It debuted at number one in the fiction category
and has been top 100 of all podcasts on Apple

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.470
Podcasts since it came out.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:52.889
Now, back to the show.

00:14:52.889 --> 00:14:56.130
[END CLIP]
The other challenge I'm having with this particular

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:01.720
chapter, because I'm sure a lot of people
who are listening or watching this will have

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:05.639
had the experience, will be having the experience,
or at some point will have the experience

00:15:05.639 --> 00:15:10.029
of hitting an impasse with some type of creative
project.

00:15:10.029 --> 00:15:13.240
Doesn't even need to be something as obvious
as say fantasy.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:16.760
It could just be a memo, internal memo to
a company.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:18.180
Maybe there are going to be layoffs, who knows?

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.170
And they're just agonizing over editing something.

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:28.740
With this particular piece, it's very complex
compared to some of the other histories.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:30.230
I've been thinking to myself like, "All right.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:33.430
Should I just try to cut this in half as an
exercise?"

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:38.190
Because maybe this one vignette is trying
to do too much, which I find very easy to

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:39.630
do if a sentence is trying to do too much.

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:40.630
I'm like, "Yeah, it's too long.

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.000
It's trying to do too much.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.480
I break it into two or three sentences or
just get rid of it."

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:52.269
When you are having any challenges in the
editing process with an area that is gummed

00:15:52.269 --> 00:15:56.360
up in whatever way, do you have any advice
for editing?

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:59.290
Steven Pressfield: Now, are you talking about,
say, a first draft, an early draft?

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:00.300
Or are you a later draft?

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:01.490
Tim Ferriss: A later draft.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.800
So it's past the first draft.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:06.990
I would say I'm probably eight or nine revisions
in.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:08.550
Steven Pressfield: Wow.

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:13.800
Tim Ferriss: One of my challenges here is,
and I think it's a sign of me being a novice,

00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:18.820
is I'm trying to establish some connective
tissue in world building as a setting for

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:20.680
what's happening.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:27.960
But I suspect that I'm doing an information
dump that is going to be hard for people to

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:31.680
digest, especially since this will probably
be in written form and in audio form.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:35.810
So especially as audio, if they can't go back
and reread a sentence, it's going to be challenging

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:37.370
for them to catch it.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:38.380
So this is a later draft.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:39.720
Steven Pressfield: Okay.

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:46.560
Let's see if I can — this is a really interesting
conversation here that I don't have.

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:51.680
My first go-to thing is, "Can I cut this?"

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:55.130
And a lot of times you can.

00:16:55.130 --> 00:17:01.600
A lot of times you can cut a lot and the audience
will accept it or the readers will accept

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:02.600
it.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:07.069
For instance, I just was watching on the airplane,
I was watching Top Gun, the Maverick one.

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:08.490
Have you seen it at all?

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:09.669
Tim Ferriss: I have, yeah.

00:17:09.669 --> 00:17:13.490
Steven Pressfield: The opening sequence, he's
in this plane, this super — it's going to

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:15.650
mach 10 or something like that.

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:21.530
And that sequence ends with the plane exploding
at 9,000 miles in the sky.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:27.689
Cut to him alive, walking into a diner in
the desert.

00:17:27.689 --> 00:17:33.830
Obviously, he's somehow bailed out and survived.

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.460
I thought, "I accept that."

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:41.650
I know from The Right Stuff that there's such
a thing as you bail out and you land in — whatever.

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:43.360
So sometimes you can really cut something.

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:44.360
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:47.250
You don't have to describe every step in between
because the audience can fill it in.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:50.289
Steven Pressfield: The other way, now the
reason I was asking is it a first draft or

00:17:50.289 --> 00:17:56.320
a later draft and it's like, I'm certainly
a believer in first drafts or early drafts,

00:17:56.320 --> 00:18:03.680
the concept of Blitzkrieg, which is the concept
of Blitzkrieg is as your tanks are rolling

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:11.330
across the enemy, whatever it is, if you encounter
an obstacle, go around it.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:16.860
Leave it behind, even if it will threaten
your supply lines and stuff like that.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:21.490
So sometimes in an early draft, if I hit a
real hard spot like that, that's driving me

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:23.559
crazy, I'll just go around it.

00:18:23.559 --> 00:18:29.310
I'll just leave it alone and let the muse
and the unconscious, because maybe a week

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:32.549
from now or two weeks from now, an idea will
come to me.

00:18:32.549 --> 00:18:33.880
"Oh, that's how I solve that."

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:37.700
Whereas if I stay there hammering at that,
I'll drive myself crazy.

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:39.730
Tim Ferriss: You'll lose momentum too.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:43.050
Steven Pressfield: Momentum I think is everything
in a case like that.

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:47.330
Tim Ferriss: So let's double down on momentum
because there's momentum in this micro sense.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:50.080
You're putting out a first draft and you hit
a bump.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:54.130
Maybe it's a name and you don't want to sit
there and just cogitate and perseverate on

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:56.330
this one name and lose all your momentum.

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:57.330
You want your tanks to keep rolling.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:58.330
Steven Pressfield: Exactly.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:18:59.360
Tim Ferriss: So you're just like, "Ah, TK,
I'll come back, whatever."

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.100
And you leave it and you keep going.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.640
Paul Rink, does that name ring a bell?

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:06.640
Steven Pressfield: Yeah.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.890
So there's also —
He's a chapter in Govt Cheese.

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:14.429
Tim Ferriss: He also, I believe, gave you
some advice as you were — was it finishing

00:19:14.429 --> 00:19:18.440
your first novel or your novel that was about
to be published?

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.669
Steven Pressfield: I think I know what you're
going to say, but keep going.

00:19:20.669 --> 00:19:24.570
Tim Ferriss: I think that he had also mentioned,
this is just an example.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:26.020
Steven Pressfield: It was close enough.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:27.020
Is that the one you're talking about?

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:28.020
Tim Ferriss: Let's see.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.940
This is actually from your website.

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:33.020
So this story in The War of Art, "...about
the afternoon when I finally, finally finished

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:34.180
my first novel manuscript…"

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:35.180
That's good.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:38.240
And then here's a word I always have trouble
saying.

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:39.240
"...after failing ignominiously…"

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:40.400
Am I getting that right?

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:41.400
Steven Pressfield: Ignominiously.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:43.960
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I always screw that up.

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.039
"...in numerous attempts over the previous
10 years.

00:19:46.039 --> 00:19:49.440
I was living in a little town in Northern
California then; I trotted down the street

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:52.270
to my friend and mentor Paul Rink and told
him the triumphant news.

00:19:52.270 --> 00:19:53.630
'Good for you,' he said without looking up.

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:55.909
'Start the next one tomorrow.'"

00:19:55.909 --> 00:19:56.929
So why did he say that?

00:19:56.929 --> 00:19:58.270
Steven Pressfield: That's another great question.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:01.250
I'm absolutely a believer in this.

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:06.110
Sometimes people will ask me, "What do you
do between books?"

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:11.780
And my answer to that is "There should never
be 'between books.'"

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:15.210
Seth Godin calls it a dip, if I understand
it.

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:19.240
The worst thing a writer or filmmaker or anybody
can do, I'm sure you know this, Tim, it's

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:24.570
like finish something, put it out there, and
then sort of wait for the world to respond.

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:25.570
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:26.570
Steven Pressfield: Right?

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:30.270
Because they're either not going to respond
at all or they're going to hate it.

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:36.210
And meanwhile you are sitting there driving
yourself insane, So I'm definitely a believer

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:41.830
that by the time I finish one book — let
me put it a different way.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:47.150
As I'm coming towards, say, the last six months
on a book, I want to be starting the next

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:48.490
book simultaneously.

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:50.530
Even if it's only notes.

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:57.110
So that when I do — like Paul Rink said
to me, "Start the next one tomorrow or today."

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:59.690
Because it's so hard to do that.

00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:03.520
And resistance is so hard, you've got to just
keep going.

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:06.640
So people will sometimes ask me too, "When
do you take a vacation?

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:07.640
When do you take a break?"

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:11.330
And the answer to that is, let's say I'm finishing
one book.

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:17.510
Okay, can I get another one sort of started
so that on Tuesday when I finish that book,

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:22.850
Wednesday, I plunge into the next one and
I'll go long enough until I have a kind of

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:27.810
beach head where I sort of know I've got the
troops are on the beach and there's enough

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:33.100
momentum that if I stop for a week or two
weeks, I'll be able to pick it up again.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:35.049
That's when I'll take a vacation.

00:21:35.049 --> 00:21:37.630
Not in between because it's so hard to get.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:40.020
So Paul Rink was a mentor to me.

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:46.059
I used to have coffee with him every morning
when I was trying to finish my first book.

00:21:46.059 --> 00:21:48.370
I was just totally focused.

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:49.450
Tim Ferriss: Was he a writer?

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:50.690
Steven Pressfield: He was a writer.

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:51.970
He died a while ago.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:55.920
He was like maybe 30 years older than me.

00:21:55.920 --> 00:22:00.270
He lived in a camper, in a pickup out in front
of his little house of his.

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:02.460
We would have coffee in his little camper
every morning.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:04.950
He sort of had taken me under his wing.

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:07.100
Tim Ferriss: Sorry, stupid question.

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:08.620
If he had a house, why was he living in his
camper?

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:09.620
Steven Pressfield: He liked the camper better.

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:10.620
What can I say?

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:11.620
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:13.270
Steven Pressfield: He would go in the house
to pee.

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:14.450
That was it.

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:21.150
But he sort of took me under his wing and
would tell me books to read and just give

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:25.650
me the writer's "psyching you up" talk each
morning.

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:28.890
Tim Ferriss: What type of advice did you find
helpful?

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:34.350
I mean I know this is a while ago, but were
there any particular pieces of advice aside

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:39.861
from the start the next one tomorrow or any
particular books you recommended, anything

00:22:39.861 --> 00:22:46.610
come to mind in terms of lessons that you
either picked up explicitly from him or things

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:47.720
that you absorbed just by —
Steven Pressfield: That's another great question,

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:48.720
Tim.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:52.390
I mean he certainly gave me many, many books
that I had to read.

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:58.230
He really had me read the canon.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:22:59.230
Tolstoy, Dostoevsky.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:00.230
All of those things.

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:01.230
Tim Ferriss: Classics.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:02.330
Steven Pressfield: Where he would just say
to me, "You've got to read, you've got to

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:03.750
know this.

00:23:03.750 --> 00:23:06.770
You can't be a writer and not know this."

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:13.900
But the other thing was that he really believed
in fiction writing as a calling.

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:17.690
It wasn't for money, it wasn't for bullshit.

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:25.200
He really made you feel like this was important
to the planet and that when you sat down and

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:29.080
do your work, you've got to do it the best
you absolutely can.

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:35.150
And the other thing that he said to me that
really helped me evolve the idea of resistance

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:43.230
was that it's a war and that you've got to
be in there every day going after it.

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:49.280
This is not a part-time occupation.

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:51.620
That it's a war and you've got to do it.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:59.679
So that was great to me at that very early
stage of trying to formulate my own ethic,

00:23:59.679 --> 00:24:05.390
coupled with that dream about ambition, you
can see how those two things are, as you're

00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:09.169
evolving as a young writer, go into your head
and help.

00:24:09.169 --> 00:24:12.039
Tim Ferriss: So a number of followup questions.

00:24:12.039 --> 00:24:19.700
The first would be why did he or why do you
feel like fiction writing as a calling is

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:21.350
important to the planet?

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:27.500
Is it because you are and each person is endowed
with certain gifts and it's your obligation

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:28.500
to share those gifts?

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:33.429
Is it because, for instance, I happen to believe
this, that truths can be transmitted sometimes

00:24:33.429 --> 00:24:36.179
much more effectively in fiction than in any
nonfiction?

00:24:36.179 --> 00:24:37.260
Steven Pressfield: Definitely.

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:38.880
Tim Ferriss: Why is it important?

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:42.460
Steven Pressfield: Well, let me back up a
little bit here, Tim, on that.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:46.309
At the time I didn't think that.

00:24:46.309 --> 00:24:49.340
At the time I was just trying to survive.

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:55.322
At this particular time when I knew Paul Rink,
I had already written one novel all the way

00:24:55.322 --> 00:24:58.200
through and then quit at the last minute.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:01.649
Blew up my marriage, blah, blah blah, my whole
life.

00:25:01.649 --> 00:25:06.029
So at this particular point where I was in,
I was working on it the second one, and my

00:25:06.029 --> 00:25:08.399
objective was just to finish this motherfucker.

00:25:08.399 --> 00:25:13.179
So I didn't have any grander — even to sell
it.

00:25:13.179 --> 00:25:14.179
Forget it.

00:25:14.179 --> 00:25:15.179
I didn't even think about it.

00:25:15.179 --> 00:25:17.990
Tim Ferriss: And so people have context, the
first one, when you stopped it right at the

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:20.370
— a hundred feet from the finish line.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.980
That was, I assume, a form of self-sabotage.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:25.580
But how did you justify it to yourself at
the time?

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:26.809
Steven Pressfield: I didn't.

00:25:26.809 --> 00:25:29.660
It was a catastrophe to me.

00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:34.950
A total failure like losing the Super Bowl
by fumbling on the one yard line.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:35.950
Tim Ferriss: "I can't finish this thing.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:36.950
I don't know how to finish it."

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:39.029
I mean, was that the internal voice at the
time?

00:25:39.029 --> 00:25:44.380
Steven Pressfield: The voice was, "Steve,
you were an idiot to even try to write a book.

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:46.520
You don't know anything about this.

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:48.470
You're not prepared mentally.

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:50.680
You've had no training, da, da, da, da.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:51.770
This is beyond you.

00:25:51.770 --> 00:25:53.360
It's like taking a tab of acid.

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:54.700
That was that big.

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:56.809
You can't handle it.

00:25:56.809 --> 00:25:58.320
You are a loser.

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:00.150
You are a bum.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:03.100
Don't ever do this again."

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:05.580
You went down one road, you've totally flamed
out.

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:09.600
Tim Ferriss: And boom, you just kind of pressed
the Looney Tunes detonation on your life at

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:10.600
the time.

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:11.600
Steven Pressfield: Exactly.

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:15.799
Tim Ferriss: So then we flash forward to Paul
Rink like, "I just want to finish this thing

00:26:15.799 --> 00:26:17.760
to prove to myself that I can finish it."

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:18.760
Steven Pressfield: Exactly.

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:26.029
And also to prove to my ex-wife that the whole
thing — because she thought I was — God

00:26:26.029 --> 00:26:30.200
knows what she thought, but I wanted to prove
to her even though that was silly.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:31.990
Tim Ferriss: I mean, that's useful fodder
then.

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:33.940
Of course, I mean you've got to use what you
can.

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.919
Steven Pressfield: All I was trying to do
was sort of get to the end of a project and

00:26:36.919 --> 00:26:43.659
just say, "I've finished it."

00:26:43.659 --> 00:26:51.110
I did finish it and for the rest of my life
I've never had any trouble finishing anything.

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:54.340
Whereas I couldn't finish anything before
and that really meant something.

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:59.740
Tim Ferriss: When you finished it, because,
I'll be honest, in my case I go through recurrent

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:03.980
bouts of finishing and not finishing.

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:06.380
So I haven't had this phase shift.

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:10.340
Although I think I complete more things than
I don't if they're worth completing.

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:11.520
Steven Pressfield: I would say you do, definitely.

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:14.200
Tim Ferriss: But the stuff that I don't finish,
no one ever sees.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:18.240
So they get this highlight reel of the things
that have been completed.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:20.270
What changed for you?

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:21.270
And was it immediate?

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:24.730
As soon as you finished were you like, "I
am now a person who finishes things," or,

00:27:24.730 --> 00:27:26.830
"I am a person who finishes things?"

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:33.980
How did it show up as a voice that, if it
did, help you, then, in future projects?

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:35.260
Steven Pressfield: It did.

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:37.490
Again, it was like the dream about ambition.

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:41.909
It did kick in right then and I knew it.

00:27:41.909 --> 00:27:43.580
I was writing on a typewriter then.

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:45.269
This was pre-computers.

00:27:45.269 --> 00:27:48.450
So when you finish, you roll the page out
of the typewriter.

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:50.310
Yeah, and you have a stack of pages.

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:51.980
You put the last page on.

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:53.750
It says "The End."

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:57.880
In those days, I had carbon paper because
you had to have a copy, that you couldn't

00:27:57.880 --> 00:27:59.360
just copy something.

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:02.540
So I really felt like "That's it.

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:04.890
I did it."

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:09.419
I didn't know that I would never have trouble
because you don't know the future.

00:28:09.419 --> 00:28:13.880
But it did feel like, "Yeah, I did something
that I couldn't do before."

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:16.110
Tim Ferriss: And that's that.

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:17.110
I've proven it to myself.

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:21.000
I have that counter example of finishing.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:25.059
Steven Pressfield: Let me ask you a question,
Tim.

00:28:25.059 --> 00:28:26.059
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:28:26.059 --> 00:28:32.019
Steven Pressfield: Is this helping you, us
talking like this in your challenges with

00:28:32.019 --> 00:28:33.019
fiction?

00:28:33.019 --> 00:28:34.019
Tim Ferriss: It is.

00:28:34.019 --> 00:28:36.070
Because as always, I'm just going on a fishing
expedition for myself.

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:38.760
So I'm looking at, for instance, momentum.

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:40.169
I felt this in my own life.

00:28:40.169 --> 00:28:41.750
So I was looking for examples of momentum.

00:28:41.750 --> 00:28:45.590
So you're talking about very macro level,
project level momentum having almost — well,

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:46.590
let's see.

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:49.210
I'll give my Hemingway comparison later.

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:58.919
But the project lead up and the note taking
setting of the table before you finish a project

00:28:58.919 --> 00:29:03.159
so that you can pick up the very next day
without having a lull.

00:29:03.159 --> 00:29:07.600
The question of when to take a break so that
you do not lose momentum.

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:12.590
And then we were talking about in the process
of drafting, say moving around on obstacles

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:14.730
again to preserve momentum.

00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:20.059
I was thinking even for me, this is the Hemingway
point, which may be apocryphal.

00:29:20.059 --> 00:29:26.750
I don't know if it's accurate, but stopping
almost mid-sentence where I know what is going

00:29:26.750 --> 00:29:27.750
to follow.

00:29:27.750 --> 00:29:31.380
So I don't sit down and agonize over what
the next few sentences are.

00:29:31.380 --> 00:29:34.040
I use that all the time and I find it really,
really helpful.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:35.420
Steven Pressfield: I agree with it completely.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:42.169
Tim Ferriss: And we're having this conversation
also a few weeks before — as we record this,

00:29:42.169 --> 00:29:47.420
it may come out a lot closer to this date
— going to be releasing this baby, which

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:50.190
is very strange, into the world.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:58.620
And so hearing you talk about charging forward
and having some type of locked and loaded

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:05.049
next step prior to waiting for the reception
that you may or may not get at all.

00:30:05.049 --> 00:30:06.450
It could be crickets.

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:07.450
It could be good.

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:11.120
It very often is going to be negative or the
negative voices will be the loudest.

00:30:11.120 --> 00:30:12.320
That's what we'll pay attention to.

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:13.799
So yes, I'm finding this out there.

00:30:13.799 --> 00:30:16.500
Steven Pressfield: Let me go back to something
we were talking about before.

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:24.039
You were asking me about, not denial, but
about having positive thoughts.

00:30:24.039 --> 00:30:29.350
Here's a couple things that just occurred
to me.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:35.000
One is, I try not to have very many friends
who are writers.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:39.419
I don't want to get into a world of writers
and I don't want to be talking with other

00:30:39.419 --> 00:30:40.419
people about it.

00:30:40.419 --> 00:30:41.419
I don't even want to know about it.

00:30:41.419 --> 00:30:46.789
And the reason I do that is when I sit down
to work on something, I want to believe I'm

00:30:46.789 --> 00:30:48.710
the only person in the world.

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:52.139
I don't want to start thinking, "Oh, shit,
I'm competing against this person.

00:30:52.139 --> 00:30:53.169
They're so smart.

00:30:53.169 --> 00:30:54.169
How am I ever…"

00:30:54.169 --> 00:30:55.340
Because that'll drive me crazy.

00:30:55.340 --> 00:31:00.380
So I keep a sort state of denial that maybe
there are three or four other writers out

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:02.019
there, but basically it's only me.

00:31:02.019 --> 00:31:05.300
And that's another thing that I definitely
want to do.

00:31:05.300 --> 00:31:11.669
And another thing is going forward and thinking
as I'm working on something, "Is this going

00:31:11.669 --> 00:31:12.960
to be any good?

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:15.450
Are people going to respond to this in any
way?"

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:20.230
I have a sense of denial or whatever where
I say, "People are going to love this.

00:31:20.230 --> 00:31:21.690
This is going to be great."

00:31:21.690 --> 00:31:25.130
And I won't let myself think about anything
else like that.

00:31:25.130 --> 00:31:30.710
I won't let myself go down any of those rabbit
holes, and I think that's also very helpful.

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:32.179
And it is delusion.

00:31:32.179 --> 00:31:40.210
Both of them are deliberate delusions that
I think you need to have in anything.

00:31:40.210 --> 00:31:45.340
If you're General Jim Mattis and you're leading
the first Marine division up to Baghdad.

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:51.769
You have to say to yourself, "I'm not going
to worry about Saddam Hussein having whatever.

00:31:51.769 --> 00:31:53.520
Our guys are just going forward."

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:57.350
Tim Ferriss: And many of my questions have
questions behind them that I'm not voicing.

00:31:57.350 --> 00:32:04.170
So I asked you about you and Paul and your
interactions and how fiction was viewed as

00:32:04.170 --> 00:32:07.649
a gift or maybe even an obligation.

00:32:07.649 --> 00:32:11.850
I'm asking in part because I'm experimenting
with fiction.

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:14.970
I get energized by it.

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:16.090
So I'm paying attention to it.

00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:17.350
There's something there.

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:19.570
There's there for me personally.

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:25.019
But at times I wonder, "Am I being self-indulgent?"

00:32:25.019 --> 00:32:29.519
A lot of what I've done in life has been from
a place of moral obligation and dealing with

00:32:29.519 --> 00:32:33.450
really, in some cases, let's just say I'm
working with therapeutics for things like

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:37.480
treatment-resistant depression, end-of-life
anxiety with people who have terminal illnesses.

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:40.210
It's very heavy.

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:46.830
So I've wanted to maybe explore how I can
try to transmit truths that are hidden like

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:50.450
Easter eggs in this fictional landscape.

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:54.100
But that's maybe another form of resistance
that's coming up for me.

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:55.360
Should I really be doing this?

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:56.779
Is this just self-indulgent?

00:32:56.779 --> 00:33:00.980
Am I just playing with toys in a corner and
not doing something that's meaningful?

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:04.029
Steven Pressfield: I don't think it is self-indulgent
at all.

00:33:04.029 --> 00:33:08.200
Let me see if I can answer that from two questions.

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:11.649
One is looking at it from the inside out.

00:33:11.649 --> 00:33:17.460
You, as a creator, as a writer, as doing something
like that, why is it important?

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:24.419
I think for our own soul, forget about anybody
else that even sees it.

00:33:24.419 --> 00:33:31.029
If you don't do that, it's when we have an
idea, we are pregnant with that idea.

00:33:31.029 --> 00:33:34.210
And that idea has a life of its own.

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:36.100
It wants to be born.

00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:41.559
And if we don't let it be born, we'll pay
the price one way or another.

00:33:41.559 --> 00:33:47.559
But I also believe that that creativity, a
dance, a song, whatever you're doing with

00:33:47.559 --> 00:33:51.080
NFTs and eight different houses or something,
whatever.

00:33:51.080 --> 00:33:57.010
I don't know what it is at all, but I know
that the world needs it.

00:33:57.010 --> 00:34:05.730
In some cosmic way, I'm definitely a believer
that there's another dimension of reality,

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:15.020
the muse or God's consciousness or whatever
that wants to bring beauty and truth into

00:34:15.020 --> 00:34:20.090
the world and that it comes through you and
me.

00:34:20.090 --> 00:34:23.760
We're sort of conduits for it, or co-creators
of it or whatever.

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:27.829
So that there is a cause — it's not self-indulgent.

00:34:27.829 --> 00:34:29.020
It would be the opposite.

00:34:29.020 --> 00:34:32.260
It's really an obligation I think we have.

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:39.710
We all have an obligation to bring that forth,
whatever it is.

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:41.649
I can't prove that that's doing any good.

00:34:41.649 --> 00:34:45.429
I mean, you look at the world, it looks pretty
screwed up.

00:34:45.429 --> 00:34:48.280
But I do think that it's not self-indulgent
at all to do that.

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:51.730
And it really is important, I think, to believe.

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:57.799
Tim Ferriss: Thank you for sharing that.

00:34:57.799 --> 00:35:02.740
As you were talking, I was thinking about
the very fundamental piece of getting so much

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:09.500
energy from doing what I'm doing because that
energy transfers outside of a writing session.

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:11.490
So it can be applied to many, many other things.

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:16.250
So it's providing overall to me more fuel
for everything that I can do.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:20.970
So that is also a reason in and of itself
to do it.

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:24.150
Steven Pressfield: Let me ask you a question
about what you're doing.

00:35:24.150 --> 00:35:29.320
Your fiction, where does that fit in? because
I know you have so many other things that

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:31.760
you're doing.

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:33.050
Where does it fit in?

00:35:33.050 --> 00:35:34.760
Is it a number one priority?

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:36.790
Is it a size thing?

00:35:36.790 --> 00:35:38.310
What is it?

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:39.520
And is it evolving?

00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:41.300
How do you see over time?

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:43.550
Tim Ferriss: I'll answer the last part first.

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:44.560
So it is evolving.

00:35:44.560 --> 00:35:50.150
If I look at my first histories that I wrote,
and actually my friends have given me feedback

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:54.040
that if who have read drafts, that if they
look at the first, it's like, "All right.

00:35:54.040 --> 00:35:56.599
Tim is really trying to find his feet."

00:35:56.599 --> 00:35:59.970
It's a little stiff.

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:06.170
And if you look at then what was written three
months later, as I'm reading classics, as

00:36:06.170 --> 00:36:12.760
I'm soaking in this, as I'm experimenting
more, as I'm getting more, I wouldn't say

00:36:12.760 --> 00:36:19.520
confident, but less constipated about how
I let things move, I think it has improved

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:21.609
a lot and that's just based on feedback.

00:36:21.609 --> 00:36:23.930
It was invisible to me because it's like the
boiling frog, right?

00:36:23.930 --> 00:36:27.150
I didn't see the degree-on-degree change.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:32.020
And to that extent, I do think it is evolving.

00:36:32.020 --> 00:36:36.319
Part of what excites me about all of this
is I don't know where it's going.

00:36:36.319 --> 00:36:41.450
I think so much of my life has been scheduled
down to the minute for decades it was kind

00:36:41.450 --> 00:36:47.730
of 15-minute increments in a Outlook calendar
and now is a Google calendar.

00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:55.950
I find that predictability to be somewhat
stifling, right?

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:59.720
It's reassuring from a safety perspective,
but it doesn't provide a lot of excitement.

00:36:59.720 --> 00:37:04.800
Not that I need excitement in the form of
disasters nonstop, but the fact that I could

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:10.250
build in some unpredictability in the form
of, say, fiction where I set the initial conditions.

00:37:10.250 --> 00:37:15.090
I set some initial scenarios, but beyond that,
I don't know where it's going.

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:16.359
It is very exciting.

00:37:16.359 --> 00:37:22.560
In terms of priority, I would say it's a very
high priority for me because it is giving

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:27.170
me so much creative energy that I am also
applying to other things.

00:37:27.170 --> 00:37:31.750
Furthermore, because I have this focus, and
this actually comes back to having a project.

00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:38.619
So for, I'd say, two years prior to that,
I decided to let there be as much negative

00:37:38.619 --> 00:37:39.690
space as possible.

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:41.720
No major projects.

00:37:41.720 --> 00:37:45.060
And in retrospect, psychologically, that was
a disaster.

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:48.600
It was not good to have that much negative
space.

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:51.460
I think there were things that came of it,
and I learned lessons including maybe you

00:37:51.460 --> 00:37:52.780
don't want that much negative space.

00:37:52.780 --> 00:37:57.300
But I had sort of let go of the trapeze without
having another one to grab onto.

00:37:57.300 --> 00:37:59.780
And I think that was a mistake.

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:05.540
And for that two-year period I really got
much less done for myself and other people

00:38:05.540 --> 00:38:12.030
than any proceeding two-year period that I
can think of.

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:16.720
Whereas now, I'm doing this fiction and I'm
spending a lot of time on it which by the

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:19.630
way, just for anybody listening does not guarantee
that you're going to like it or it's going

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:25.290
to be good, but it's giving something to me
that is helping me to be better at everything

00:38:25.290 --> 00:38:26.520
else than I'm doing.

00:38:26.520 --> 00:38:31.220
Case in point, we're having this conversation
right now, and we can actually have many portions

00:38:31.220 --> 00:38:33.540
of this conversation because I'm working on
it.

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:35.270
And you are a domain expert.

00:38:35.270 --> 00:38:37.359
You have so much time in the trenches.

00:38:37.359 --> 00:38:39.069
So there's a direct carryover.

00:38:39.069 --> 00:38:43.890
I mean, it's not accidental that I wanted
to have this conversation with you, but it's

00:38:43.890 --> 00:38:45.180
helping other things.

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:47.070
So there's that.

00:38:47.070 --> 00:38:54.210
I think I'm kind of dodging the question a
little bit because I don't know if I can confidently

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:59.060
say it's my number one priority, but it is
absolutely from a creative project perspective

00:38:59.060 --> 00:39:03.410
what I'm paying the most attention to right
now.

00:39:03.410 --> 00:39:04.860
Why do you ask?

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:12.000
Steven Pressfield: Because when you've tentatively
brought up in our last discussion, I kind

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:13.060
of said, "Are you working on fiction?"

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:18.100
Which I didn't have any idea that you were,
and you lit up at that.

00:39:18.100 --> 00:39:23.690
I really felt like I'd hit an electric circuit
with that.

00:39:23.690 --> 00:39:24.730
I've been wondering.

00:39:24.730 --> 00:39:26.490
Where does that fit in Tim's head?

00:39:26.490 --> 00:39:28.300
Is he going in that direction?

00:39:28.300 --> 00:39:32.390
Because he's got so many other things that
I know are important projects that I know

00:39:32.390 --> 00:39:36.079
are important to you, things you care about
for the planet and for everything like that.

00:39:36.079 --> 00:39:39.751
So I was just really wondering where that
fit in, where fiction fit in.

00:39:39.751 --> 00:39:44.890
Tim Ferriss: I feel like fiction is fuel for
the other things that consume fuel.

00:39:44.890 --> 00:39:45.940
Steven Pressfield: Interesting.

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:51.160
Tim Ferriss: It's adding to the black side
of the ledger, whereas a lot of these other

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:52.680
things are important, but —
Steven Pressfield: Energetically.

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:55.760
Tim Ferriss: Energetically, they feel like
debits.

00:39:55.760 --> 00:40:01.470
And I haven't had something to countervail
that depletion.

00:40:01.470 --> 00:40:05.580
But these days, man, it's like I wake up,
I'm excited to jump into this stuff.

00:40:05.580 --> 00:40:09.640
It's been a long time since I felt this excited.

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:14.490
I could see it informing a lot of what I do
in the future.

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:21.980
And also on the same walk I was mentioning
earlier with these guys, a number of us repeatedly,

00:40:21.980 --> 00:40:26.420
someone brought it up and then all of us quickly
agreed that it seems like a lot of us were

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:33.630
all — I think we were all past the age of
40, I'm 45, that maybe around 40, a lot of

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:41.490
these guys who are very good in their respective
fields had come back to what made them joyful

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:45.040
as 13- or 14-year-olds.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:46.790
At that time, what was I doing?

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:48.420
I wanted to be a color book penciler.

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:54.740
I was drawing all the time and I was creating
fictional worlds in the form of comic books

00:40:54.740 --> 00:41:00.640
because I was basically creating panels and
doing storyboarding for the movie of my mind,

00:41:00.640 --> 00:41:03.140
which at the time took the form of comic books.

00:41:03.140 --> 00:41:08.490
So in a sense, this is returning to the source
of a lot of that joy and energy as a kid that

00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:12.390
I just lost sight of because I viewed it as
childish, self-indulgent.

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:13.700
This is what kids do.

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:16.690
Now, I'm supposed to be an adult.

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:19.560
I threw the baby out with the bath water.

00:41:19.560 --> 00:41:22.640
So it fits in a very fundamental way.

00:41:22.640 --> 00:41:31.790
It's just that it took a long time for me
to maybe reclaim or resurrect that piece.

00:41:31.790 --> 00:41:37.609
Steven Pressfield: Let's look at this for
a second from the point of view of the other

00:41:37.609 --> 00:41:46.599
dimensions of reality or from the muse's point
of view that somehow in you there's an underground

00:41:46.599 --> 00:41:52.079
river flowing from childhood, from doing that
sort of stuff.

00:41:52.079 --> 00:41:56.660
And obviously now, it's like when you tell
me about eight different houses, I say, where

00:41:56.660 --> 00:41:57.950
is this coming from?

00:41:57.950 --> 00:42:03.410
I mean it's not something that you just sit
down, "Oh, let me do this giant worlds of

00:42:03.410 --> 00:42:05.890
different time dimensions and everything."

00:42:05.890 --> 00:42:08.560
That's coming from somewhere.

00:42:08.560 --> 00:42:12.520
If we were going to look at it from the point
of view of the Greek gods, then there would

00:42:12.520 --> 00:42:18.300
be a muse that's got all this stuff, all this
papers in front of her and saying, "I'm giving

00:42:18.300 --> 00:42:19.300
these to Tim."

00:42:19.300 --> 00:42:25.950
So if you don't do that, and then the fact
that your energized by that and the fact that

00:42:25.950 --> 00:42:31.880
when you don't do that, you're being depleted.

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:39.099
That sort of gives a picture of reality where
you are sort of a receptor of flow of energy

00:42:39.099 --> 00:42:40.180
coming from somewhere else.

00:42:40.180 --> 00:42:47.260
And it's not just random energy, it's very
definitely a world, a story, a universe, a

00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:53.010
cosmos that you or I, or anybody doing that
we can't know what it means.

00:42:53.010 --> 00:42:55.180
Does it help anybody?

00:42:55.180 --> 00:42:56.339
And it does seem self-indulgent.

00:42:56.339 --> 00:42:58.950
Why am I just drawing these pictures?

00:42:58.950 --> 00:42:59.970
But it isn't.

00:42:59.970 --> 00:43:02.400
In some way it isn't.

00:43:02.400 --> 00:43:06.400
And in fact it's the most important thing.

00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:12.369
Everything we learn in this commercial world
says the opposite to that.

00:43:12.369 --> 00:43:18.809
You should be seriously working on, when in
fact, what this is that you're working on,

00:43:18.809 --> 00:43:24.540
the story you're working on, in my mind, it's
probably the most important thing.

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:29.920
Maybe I'm prejudiced because I don't know
other stuff, but I think that's — and that's

00:43:29.920 --> 00:43:31.470
true for everybody.

00:43:31.470 --> 00:43:39.619
If we don't do it, that underground river
is going to fuck us up one way or another

00:43:39.619 --> 00:43:41.609
if we don't let it flow.

00:43:41.609 --> 00:43:47.310
Tim Ferriss: As you're mentioning this it
coming from somewhere, part of what has been,

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:53.980
so I think rejuvenating about this for me
is that if I sit down, and this is not meant

00:43:53.980 --> 00:43:57.930
as a slight against nonfiction, because I
love nonfiction.

00:43:57.930 --> 00:43:58.940
I read nonfiction.

00:43:58.940 --> 00:44:04.890
I've written a lot of nonfiction, but at times,
not always, but a lot of the time it can feel

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:05.890
like carpentry.

00:44:05.890 --> 00:44:06.890
You're like, "All right.

00:44:06.890 --> 00:44:08.460
I've got to lay these bricks.

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:10.250
There are 1,200 of them.

00:44:10.250 --> 00:44:11.819
Here we go."

00:44:11.819 --> 00:44:14.230
And you know what the wall is going to look
like.

00:44:14.230 --> 00:44:16.770
You have to know what the wall is going to
look like in most cases.

00:44:16.770 --> 00:44:21.829
Whereas with this, there have been a number
of these vignettes where, it doesn't happen

00:44:21.829 --> 00:44:24.150
every morning, it doesn't happen every day.

00:44:24.150 --> 00:44:33.099
But when I sit down to write and it just comes
out, it all just kind of lands almost like

00:44:33.099 --> 00:44:35.170
a finished piece.

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:39.660
There's revision, everything's going to fall,
but all of these unusual story elements and

00:44:39.660 --> 00:44:43.180
characters just kind of appear.

00:44:43.180 --> 00:44:44.610
I'm not pausing at all.

00:44:44.610 --> 00:44:50.809
And that is a feeling that is very hard for
me to capture in words, maybe ironically.

00:44:50.809 --> 00:44:57.359
But that flow state, which is a term that's
overused, which is why I'm grasping for maybe

00:44:57.359 --> 00:45:04.180
another way to put it, but that dancing with
the muse, that feeling of being a conduit

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:07.330
for something, which I don't feel in many
other places.

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:09.580
There are a few.

00:45:09.580 --> 00:45:15.060
Maybe in psychedelic experiences, maybe in
certain sexual experiences where something

00:45:15.060 --> 00:45:18.960
is happening through you as opposed to you
doing something.

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:23.070
I mean, this project is going to be absurd
and really strange.

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:28.200
So I don't want to make it sound like I'm
hatching the next Tolkien masterpiece.

00:45:28.200 --> 00:45:29.200
But that doesn't matter.

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:33.240
It's kind of beside the point because it's
the feeling that I'm paying attention to and

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:34.670
it's so unusual.

00:45:34.670 --> 00:45:37.650
And it gives me so much endurance.

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:43.010
I'm like, "Okay, there's something here that
is worth paying attention to.

00:45:43.010 --> 00:45:45.690
I don't need to be able to explain it.

00:45:45.690 --> 00:45:51.690
I don't need to be able to even describe it
necessarily, but it's feeding me in a way

00:45:51.690 --> 00:45:52.930
that I haven't been fed."

00:45:52.930 --> 00:45:55.579
Steven Pressfield: It's fascinating, isn't
it?

00:45:55.579 --> 00:45:56.579
Tim Ferriss: It's super fascinating.

00:45:56.579 --> 00:45:59.140
Steven Pressfield: I mean, they don't teach
you this in school.

00:45:59.140 --> 00:46:03.960
This is not the reality that they tell you
is what the world is all about.

00:46:03.960 --> 00:46:07.369
But it is what the world is all about for
certain kinds of people.

00:46:07.369 --> 00:46:08.369
Tim Ferriss: Definitely.

00:46:08.369 --> 00:46:15.180
So there's this experience, say, of popping
up on the wave and just riding it perfectly

00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:17.380
and being in that flow state, metaphorically
speaking.

00:46:17.380 --> 00:46:22.290
And then there's the work and there's the
regimentation.

00:46:22.290 --> 00:46:27.700
I'd love for you to tell the story of, and
I have a lot of pages here.

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:31.339
Usually, I have my fancy clipboard, but I
had so many notes for this conversation.

00:46:31.339 --> 00:46:33.930
I was like, "This isn't even going to work
if I had a clipboard.

00:46:33.930 --> 00:46:34.930
I've got 20 pages here."

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:35.930
Let me find, and I could probably just prompt
you.

00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:36.930
You know what?

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:45.030
I can't find it here, but when you got your
job as a driver, trucking, delivering, there

00:46:45.030 --> 00:46:50.140
was a conversation I want to say that your
boss at the time or soon-to-be boss had with

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:51.430
you.

00:46:51.430 --> 00:46:56.550
It seemed like that was a slap in the face
that sort of changed your orientation towards

00:46:56.550 --> 00:46:58.200
work, at least up to that point.

00:46:58.200 --> 00:46:59.360
Can you tell that story please?

00:46:59.360 --> 00:47:02.900
Steven Pressfield: And this is all in Govt
Cheese, by the way.

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:07.710
I really wanted to tell those stories in detail.

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:12.820
And Tim, you actually, when you were asking
me these questions on our last conversation,

00:47:12.820 --> 00:47:15.450
that was sort of what inspired me to actually
write this book.

00:47:15.450 --> 00:47:17.660
Tim Ferriss: We can hit all that first too.

00:47:17.660 --> 00:47:21.369
So let's actually just hit that and then we
can talk about this conversation.

00:47:21.369 --> 00:47:23.900
But you can write a million different things.

00:47:23.900 --> 00:47:27.510
Why write Govt Cheese, and why the title Govt
Cheese?

00:47:27.510 --> 00:47:33.460
Steven Pressfield: Well, it's a memoir, and
like I say, when we had our last conversation

00:47:33.460 --> 00:47:40.089
and you started asking me about specifics
in my life, how did you go from it where you

00:47:40.089 --> 00:47:43.800
were there to where you were there, you were
sort of interested in the creative process

00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:45.309
and the whole thing.

00:47:45.309 --> 00:47:47.520
And I thought for the first time that — I
thought about telling those stories and writing

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:51.040
that all down at some point, but I thought
nobody's going to be interested in this.

00:47:51.040 --> 00:47:55.579
But the fact that you were, and then I did
a podcast with Rich Roll and he was interested

00:47:55.579 --> 00:47:56.710
in it too.

00:47:56.710 --> 00:48:03.320
And with Diana, my significant other who you
just met, she also was all about that.

00:48:03.320 --> 00:48:04.410
Tim Ferriss: And she's like, "Wait a minute."

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:07.790
Steven Pressfield: I thought I'd better do
this and that maybe my resistance to this

00:48:07.790 --> 00:48:09.400
is pure resistance.

00:48:09.400 --> 00:48:16.631
So what I did want to do was tell the real
stories of my evolution as a writer and my

00:48:16.631 --> 00:48:21.829
years in the wilderness, struggling doing
other jobs and not succeeding, et cetera,

00:48:21.829 --> 00:48:22.829
et cetera.

00:48:22.829 --> 00:48:24.820
So I thought, "Let me write a fucking memoir.

00:48:24.820 --> 00:48:27.650
Let me just tell the true story."

00:48:27.650 --> 00:48:31.970
Because I thought it would be helpful to other
people who were on that odyssey.

00:48:31.970 --> 00:48:34.080
That was what sort of tipped me over into
doing it.

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:36.960
Of course, I wanted to do it myself or my
own reasons.

00:48:36.960 --> 00:48:41.410
But anyway, so back to this particular story
that you were asking me about.

00:48:41.410 --> 00:48:42.940
I'll give you the long version.

00:48:42.940 --> 00:48:43.940
You can edit whatever you want.

00:48:43.940 --> 00:48:47.089
Tim Ferriss: This is long form, so we're in
the perfect place for long story.

00:48:47.089 --> 00:48:53.920
Steven Pressfield: So by luck, I had gotten
a job as a long-haul tractor trailer driver.

00:48:53.920 --> 00:49:01.200
I was the youngest guy at a trucking company
and hanging on by my fingernails and trying

00:49:01.200 --> 00:49:06.809
not to screw up because self-sabotage was
the thing in my — and the short version

00:49:06.809 --> 00:49:11.260
of it was I dropped a trailer.

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:21.690
$300,000 worth, 40,000 pounds of textile machinery
— I pulled out from under a park trailer,

00:49:21.690 --> 00:49:26.730
not knowing that I hadn't coupled — I think
crashed to the deck.

00:49:26.730 --> 00:49:32.340
It was just a total fuck up that I felt was
also self-sabotage.

00:49:32.340 --> 00:49:35.190
It was me trying to screw myself up.

00:49:35.190 --> 00:49:37.740
So I had a boss, his name was Hugh Reeves.

00:49:37.740 --> 00:49:41.750
One of the — book number one in here is
called "Hugh Reeves" because he was such a

00:49:41.750 --> 00:49:43.750
great mentor to me.

00:49:43.750 --> 00:49:46.579
So he didn't fire me immediately.

00:49:46.579 --> 00:49:53.160
He didn't fire me anyway, but he took me out
to a hot dog place in Durham, North Carolina

00:49:53.160 --> 00:50:00.059
for lunch and he said to me, he said, "Son,
I can tell that you're going through something

00:50:00.059 --> 00:50:03.670
in your mind, that you're living out some
kind of issues."

00:50:03.670 --> 00:50:05.920
He says, "I don't want to know what they are.

00:50:05.920 --> 00:50:07.849
I don't give a shit what they are.

00:50:07.849 --> 00:50:11.130
Just remember you drive for me.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:16.099
This company is a commercial enterprise designed
to make money.

00:50:16.099 --> 00:50:18.280
You're not living out any odyssey here."

00:50:18.280 --> 00:50:26.030
And he said, "I hired you to deliver a fucking
load and you better deliver it every time.

00:50:26.030 --> 00:50:28.110
You're a professional driver.

00:50:28.110 --> 00:50:32.200
Do it."

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:38.000
So that really was something, again, that
stuck with me from that moment on and was

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:41.760
reflected in writing, in learning how to write.

00:50:41.760 --> 00:50:43.070
This is not a joke.

00:50:43.070 --> 00:50:45.600
"You're a professional person.

00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:47.089
Do it.

00:50:47.089 --> 00:50:48.090
Whatever it takes.

00:50:48.090 --> 00:50:49.099
Do it."

00:50:49.099 --> 00:50:52.570
And so that has lived with me forever since
then.

00:50:52.570 --> 00:50:57.080
And that was a great mentor situation, Hugh
Reeves, wherever you are.

00:50:57.080 --> 00:50:58.930
Tim Ferriss: And he was former military?

00:50:58.930 --> 00:51:00.050
Steven Pressfield: Yeah.

00:51:00.050 --> 00:51:03.990
He was a Marine from a few generations before
me.

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:06.819
Tim Ferriss: Did you have military background
as well yourself?

00:51:06.819 --> 00:51:08.819
Steven Pressfield: I mean, I was a Marine
reservist.

00:51:08.819 --> 00:51:13.740
I was never in combat, but I was an infantryman
in the training.

00:51:13.740 --> 00:51:15.700
Tim Ferriss: And the wilderness.

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:19.579
So you mentioned the wilderness.

00:51:19.579 --> 00:51:22.549
What is the sojourn in the wilderness?

00:51:22.549 --> 00:51:23.990
And can you skip it?

00:51:23.990 --> 00:51:26.900
Steven Pressfield: I don't think you can.

00:51:26.900 --> 00:51:31.329
I mean, I'm doing a little thing on Instagram.

00:51:31.329 --> 00:51:36.289
As you know I'm sure, called — a little
series.

00:51:36.289 --> 00:51:37.670
I think we all have a passage.

00:51:37.670 --> 00:51:39.119
Tim Ferriss: "In the Wilderness," for people
who want to —

00:51:39.119 --> 00:51:44.970
Steven Pressfield: "In the Wilderness," meaning
the bottom falls out of our life.

00:51:44.970 --> 00:51:47.190
Maybe it's addiction.

00:51:47.190 --> 00:51:49.750
Maybe it's PTSD.

00:51:49.750 --> 00:51:55.520
Maybe it's abuse of others or abuse of ourselves.

00:51:55.520 --> 00:52:02.350
For me it was like a geographic odyssey where
I was working hard labor jobs in crazy parts

00:52:02.350 --> 00:52:06.161
of the country that I never thought I would
ever be in and I couldn't get out of this

00:52:06.161 --> 00:52:08.270
thing.

00:52:08.270 --> 00:52:14.339
I do think that we all need that passage somehow.

00:52:14.339 --> 00:52:22.349
Like The Odyssey, I know I'm probably getting
way too deep into this, but Homer's Odyssey,

00:52:22.349 --> 00:52:25.859
the story of Odysseus returning from the Trojan
War.

00:52:25.859 --> 00:52:31.190
10 years, he's blown hither and thither about,
trying to get home.

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:39.880
That to me is of the granddaddy of all hero's
journeys in Western civilization, anyway.

00:52:39.880 --> 00:52:46.410
The reason that The Odyssey still is so powerful
3,000 years after it was written is because

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:47.589
it's all in our blood.

00:52:47.589 --> 00:52:49.470
It's in our bones.

00:52:49.470 --> 00:52:50.569
We all have that.

00:52:50.569 --> 00:52:52.650
I mean, it can happen.

00:52:52.650 --> 00:52:58.110
You can go to jail and that's your wilderness
time.

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:00.329
We all sort of need that, I think.

00:53:00.329 --> 00:53:04.970
And this book, Govt Cheese, is my time in
the wilderness.

00:53:04.970 --> 00:53:08.760
And like I say, the reason I wanted to put
it on paper was because I thought it might

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:13.890
help other people because one of the other
things about — I know I'm blathering on

00:53:13.890 --> 00:53:15.510
here, Tim, but we've got a lot of tape.

00:53:15.510 --> 00:53:17.299
Tim Ferriss: We've got all the time in the
world.

00:53:17.299 --> 00:53:21.640
Steven Pressfield: One of the factors, one
of the characteristics of a time in the wilderness

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:29.569
in my experience is that we're blind to its
significance while we're in it.

00:53:29.569 --> 00:53:32.180
We think it's meaningless.

00:53:32.180 --> 00:53:33.890
We think our life is meaningless.

00:53:33.890 --> 00:53:35.540
We're lost, right?

00:53:35.540 --> 00:53:40.660
We don't have any concept of why we're doing
what we're doing, why we're fucking up like

00:53:40.660 --> 00:53:42.220
we are.

00:53:42.220 --> 00:53:46.730
But what I'm trying to say is there is meaning
to it.

00:53:46.730 --> 00:53:51.619
Again, it's that sort of other dimension of
reality that we were talking about that that

00:53:51.619 --> 00:53:53.910
dimension is wiser than we are.

00:53:53.910 --> 00:54:01.609
It has sent us on this passage, I believe,
to teach us.

00:54:01.609 --> 00:54:06.950
Every passage to the wilderness, like The
Odyssey, is a journey toward home, and home

00:54:06.950 --> 00:54:09.690
meaning who we really are.

00:54:09.690 --> 00:54:15.850
We're trying to find our authentic self where
we can say, "Ah, this is what I should be

00:54:15.850 --> 00:54:18.170
doing that I can't do.

00:54:18.170 --> 00:54:22.150
That I can't do, but this is where I should
be.

00:54:22.150 --> 00:54:23.760
This is the lane I should be in."

00:54:23.760 --> 00:54:25.180
And that's home.

00:54:25.180 --> 00:54:28.599
And it takes a while to find it.

00:54:28.599 --> 00:54:36.110
Tim Ferriss: As I'm listening, it makes me
think that those — I mean not exactly, but

00:54:36.110 --> 00:54:40.369
pretty close to the last two years that I
mentioned, which were fucking awful.

00:54:40.369 --> 00:54:45.579
I've got to be honest, in the moment, I was
just like, "I'm lost.

00:54:45.579 --> 00:54:47.220
I don't know what I'm supposed to do.

00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:48.520
Maybe I'm not supposed to do anything.

00:54:48.520 --> 00:54:49.609
Maybe this is it.

00:54:49.609 --> 00:54:52.339
I'm generally feeling pretty depressive.

00:54:52.339 --> 00:54:54.240
What the fuck is this?

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:56.070
I don't know how to find my way out of this.

00:54:56.070 --> 00:54:58.990
I don't know anyone who's been able to tell
me how to get out of this."

00:54:58.990 --> 00:55:03.069
Steven Pressfield: How did you get out of
it?

00:55:03.069 --> 00:55:04.210
Was there an event?

00:55:04.210 --> 00:55:06.370
Was there a time, a moment?

00:55:06.370 --> 00:55:14.539
Tim Ferriss: I think it was a decision to
— well, I don't want to take too much credit

00:55:14.539 --> 00:55:15.539
for it.

00:55:15.539 --> 00:55:16.539
Steven Pressfield: No, go ahead.

00:55:16.539 --> 00:55:17.539
Take credit.

00:55:17.539 --> 00:55:24.550
Tim Ferriss: Being exposed to a bunch of absurdity
and comedy and realizing how much meaning

00:55:24.550 --> 00:55:30.510
I was able to — enjoyment and joy and laughter,
I was able to take out of that, but also how

00:55:30.510 --> 00:55:34.480
much profundity can be hidden in the absurd.

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:36.599
Steven Pressfield: Was this a psychedelic
experience?

00:55:36.599 --> 00:55:41.920
Tim Ferriss: I mean, certainly I could describe
some of my psychedelic experiences that way.

00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:46.630
It was also seeing what a number of friends
were doing in the NFT space, which is just,

00:55:46.630 --> 00:55:48.600
on its face in every way, fucking ridiculous.

00:55:48.600 --> 00:55:53.910
I mean, it's so silly on so many levels.

00:55:53.910 --> 00:55:56.740
And yet I saw people finding meaning.

00:55:56.740 --> 00:56:00.780
I saw people creating incredible artwork.

00:56:00.780 --> 00:56:06.990
And so that prompted me to begin to ask, and
this was really at some of the darkest moments

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:11.150
to say, "Okay, I've tried to do everything
very seriously.

00:56:11.150 --> 00:56:12.950
I've tried that.

00:56:12.950 --> 00:56:15.829
It doesn't seem to be really working for me."

00:56:15.829 --> 00:56:17.400
It's produced some amazing things.

00:56:17.400 --> 00:56:19.190
I'm very proud of those things.

00:56:19.190 --> 00:56:21.510
But net-net, it's a hard slog.

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:26.809
It's just like holding a heavy backpack over
my head and walking through mud that's waist

00:56:26.809 --> 00:56:29.070
deep all the time.

00:56:29.070 --> 00:56:31.010
Maybe I don't have to do it that way.

00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:32.780
Maybe I can walk around the swamp.

00:56:32.780 --> 00:56:36.270
Maybe there's a hang glider, a zip line that
I can take across.

00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:39.580
I just decided that that's cheating and I
have to do it the hard way.

00:56:39.580 --> 00:56:44.619
And that's when I started to think about what
I could do that would simply — because one

00:56:44.619 --> 00:56:52.030
of my experiences, and it's hard to say which
way the arrow of causality points with this,

00:56:52.030 --> 00:57:02.280
but any type of depression or experience of
melancholy is always accommodated with a feeling

00:57:02.280 --> 00:57:04.990
of fatigue, lethargy.

00:57:04.990 --> 00:57:14.060
So the fight, for me, the goal, the life raft
that I'm looking for is energy.

00:57:14.060 --> 00:57:18.020
Wherever I can get energy, if that's jumping
rope for five minutes, if that's jumping into

00:57:18.020 --> 00:57:25.040
an ice cold bath, if it's waking up at a certain
time, if it's drinking less coffee after 2:00

00:57:25.040 --> 00:57:30.750
p.m. because I need to go to sleep, everything
in my life begins to revolve around thinking

00:57:30.750 --> 00:57:33.829
about energy.

00:57:33.829 --> 00:57:42.880
What I found was the more I engaged with absurdity
and fiction, which could be visual products

00:57:42.880 --> 00:57:47.250
of the imagination, let's put it that way,
products of the imagination with no obvious

00:57:47.250 --> 00:57:51.690
practical application, the more energy I had.

00:57:51.690 --> 00:57:52.690
Steven Pressfield: Interesting.

00:57:52.690 --> 00:57:54.550
Tim Ferriss: And I was like, "Okay.

00:57:54.550 --> 00:57:59.059
Well, if that's what's presenting itself,
let me keep focusing on that."

00:57:59.059 --> 00:58:05.920
And then once I began to — and this might
overlap in a way with your story about — what

00:58:05.920 --> 00:58:07.099
was his name again, your mentor?

00:58:07.099 --> 00:58:08.099
Steven Pressfield: Paul Rink.

00:58:08.099 --> 00:58:09.099
Tim Ferriss: Not Paul.

00:58:09.099 --> 00:58:10.099
Steven Pressfield: Hugh Reeves.

00:58:10.099 --> 00:58:11.099
Tim Ferriss: Hugh.

00:58:11.099 --> 00:58:14.319
So this might overlap with Hugh in a way because
as soon as I then started to engage other

00:58:14.319 --> 00:58:22.720
people to help me with creating, say, art,
now I had people who are depending on me or

00:58:22.720 --> 00:58:24.480
that I was depending on.

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:26.160
Now I had some accountability.

00:58:26.160 --> 00:58:28.030
Now there are things on the calendar.

00:58:28.030 --> 00:58:38.170
There was some structure around this free-floating
desire to use fiction to energize myself.

00:58:38.170 --> 00:58:40.690
But now I had some scaffolding.

00:58:40.690 --> 00:58:44.430
And I think that is in large part what pulled
me out.

00:58:44.430 --> 00:58:45.910
It was that.

00:58:45.910 --> 00:58:50.539
Once I had enough energy, and this is a chicken
or the egg kind of thing, but once I had just

00:58:50.539 --> 00:58:56.559
a basic modicum of energy from engaging with
absurdity — and I don't use absurdity.

00:58:56.559 --> 00:59:04.131
I mentioned this word online recently because
there was a Camus quote, which was something

00:59:04.131 --> 00:59:08.990
like, "Absurdity and happiness are brothers
arm in arm.

00:59:08.990 --> 00:59:10.630
You cannot have one without the other."

00:59:10.630 --> 00:59:11.630
Something like that.

00:59:11.630 --> 00:59:16.319
I'm mangling the quote, but I saw a number
of people reply to that saying, "What is wrong

00:59:16.319 --> 00:59:17.320
with happiness?

00:59:17.320 --> 00:59:19.680
Why do you need to make it negative with absurdity?"

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:24.770
And that in and of itself seemed absurd to
me because I was like, "That's not what I'm

00:59:24.770 --> 00:59:25.770
saying.

00:59:25.770 --> 00:59:27.250
I don't think that's what he was saying."

00:59:27.250 --> 00:59:35.109
And in fact, absurdity, paradox, I more and
more think, is this incredible ingredient

00:59:35.109 --> 00:59:36.430
of the human condition.

00:59:36.430 --> 00:59:37.530
I know I'm talking a lot.

00:59:37.530 --> 00:59:39.079
I'll stop my TED Talk in a second.

00:59:39.079 --> 00:59:46.490
But the lift up that the absurdity gave me
then provided enough energy that I could begin

00:59:46.490 --> 00:59:47.790
exercising regularly.

00:59:47.790 --> 00:59:48.790
Now what happens?

00:59:48.790 --> 00:59:54.980
Now, I have this flywheel of exercise and
absurdity that is charging my batteries.

00:59:54.980 --> 00:59:59.710
So I think if I had to explain it, and maybe
I can't explain it.

00:59:59.710 --> 01:00:02.530
Maybe it just happened because one day I woke
up and my neurochemistry was different because

01:00:02.530 --> 01:00:03.900
I had four nights of good sleep.

01:00:03.900 --> 01:00:05.040
Who knows?

01:00:05.040 --> 01:00:06.940
But that's how I would say I got out of it.

01:00:06.940 --> 01:00:08.520
Steven Pressfield: Ah, pretty good.

01:00:08.520 --> 01:00:11.030
Let me take a shot at absurdity for a second.

01:00:11.030 --> 01:00:13.410
I'm just thinking this as we're talking.

01:00:13.410 --> 01:00:18.220
Any time something is brand new, it seems
absurd.

01:00:18.220 --> 01:00:22.079
Because I'm sure when Columbus said, "Let
me sail west."

01:00:22.079 --> 01:00:23.369
Was it east?

01:00:23.369 --> 01:00:26.270
Let me say west, to go to the East Indies."

01:00:26.270 --> 01:00:27.660
Everybody said, "That's absurd."

01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:28.660
Right?

01:00:28.660 --> 01:00:29.870
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:00:29.870 --> 01:00:32.020
Steven Pressfield: But it wasn't absurd, was
it?

01:00:32.020 --> 01:00:33.020
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:00:33.020 --> 01:00:38.700
Steven Pressfield: Or whatever you are working
in on fiction now, because it's new, and I

01:00:38.700 --> 01:00:43.660
guess NFTs come into this too, they seem absurd
because they're not what we know.

01:00:43.660 --> 01:00:46.430
They're the unknown, but they're not absurd.

01:00:46.430 --> 01:00:50.109
They're just one step into the unknown.

01:00:50.109 --> 01:00:56.620
I can say when I first had the idea for The
Legend of Bagger Vance or for Gates of Fire,

01:00:56.620 --> 01:01:01.480
both of them, I thought, "These are the dumbest
ideas I've ever had in terms of — who's

01:01:01.480 --> 01:01:03.819
going to be interested in this?"

01:01:03.819 --> 01:01:07.310
So it did seem absurd, but it wasn't absurd.

01:01:07.310 --> 01:01:10.809
So absurd is a word that has negative connotations.

01:01:10.809 --> 01:01:16.020
It should be another word, but I think that's
what Camus must have meant, not knowing what

01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:17.020
the quote was.

01:01:17.020 --> 01:01:18.020
Tim Ferriss: I think it is.

01:01:18.020 --> 01:01:19.020
Steven Pressfield: So it's positive, I think.

01:01:19.020 --> 01:01:20.020
It's just new.

01:01:20.020 --> 01:01:21.020
Tim Ferriss: I think it's positive.

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:25.900
I take it as very positive, or at the very
worst, neutral.

01:01:25.900 --> 01:01:34.270
This makes me think of something that I highlighted
from our last conversation, which is — and

01:01:34.270 --> 01:01:35.270
this is the note that I have.

01:01:35.270 --> 01:01:39.390
Regarding how to start SP, that's you, acknowledges
that Tim will need to learn the principles

01:01:39.390 --> 01:01:40.390
of writing fiction.

01:01:40.390 --> 01:01:43.060
Nevertheless, he thinks Tim should just dive
in with something he loves and follow it.

01:01:43.060 --> 01:01:45.581
And then this is a quote from you, "Say to
yourself, when I show this to people, they're

01:01:45.581 --> 01:01:48.480
going to look at me and go, 'What happened
to you, Tim?

01:01:48.480 --> 01:01:49.779
Are you okay?'"

01:01:49.779 --> 01:01:50.970
That's what I mean by big.

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:54.740
And I've used that as a north star of sorts.

01:01:54.740 --> 01:01:58.921
It's like if it's too comfortable, if people
aren't going to look at me and go, "Are you

01:01:58.921 --> 01:01:59.921
okay?"

01:01:59.921 --> 01:02:04.970
Then I'm probably playing it too safe is how
I looked at it.

01:02:04.970 --> 01:02:08.780
I mean so far, good Lord, it has really — I
mean, it's paying off so far.

01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:13.880
I've said, also, to many people because this
thing is going to launch and when it does,

01:02:13.880 --> 01:02:15.240
what'll happen will happen.

01:02:15.240 --> 01:02:23.140
But what I've said, and I've maybe for the
first time really feel that I can say with

01:02:23.140 --> 01:02:27.660
a straight face is that I've already gotten
all I need to get out of this.

01:02:27.660 --> 01:02:30.180
I don't care how it's received at all.

01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:31.610
Steven Pressfield: I'm sure that's true.

01:02:31.610 --> 01:02:40.589
Tim Ferriss: Just by reactivating and embracing
these deep elements of myself that I've forced

01:02:40.589 --> 01:02:42.240
to be dormant for so long.

01:02:42.240 --> 01:02:46.200
Oh, man, it's all icing on the cake from here.

01:02:46.200 --> 01:02:50.559
Steven Pressfield: Let me take a shot at your
bad two years.

01:02:50.559 --> 01:02:51.869
Tim Ferriss: Please.

01:02:51.869 --> 01:02:53.609
Yeah, that was terrible.

01:02:53.609 --> 01:02:56.720
Steven Pressfield: Let's try this as an interpretation.

01:02:56.720 --> 01:03:00.710
This underground river that we were talking
about of creativity that goes back from your

01:03:00.710 --> 01:03:07.520
comic book drawing days when you were a kid
has been flowing through you all along.

01:03:07.520 --> 01:03:12.200
It kind of built up to a head maybe two years
ago or whenever the time was where you went

01:03:12.200 --> 01:03:14.090
into your period of the wilderness.

01:03:14.090 --> 01:03:19.510
And for whatever reason you should have started
it then.

01:03:19.510 --> 01:03:28.599
But probably because of fear, possibly just
ignorance, blocking it out somehow.

01:03:28.599 --> 01:03:30.670
Instead, you came up with another thing.

01:03:30.670 --> 01:03:33.680
Let me go into — how did you describe it?

01:03:33.680 --> 01:03:40.369
Not a fallow period, but a period of — an
offline period or something like that.

01:03:40.369 --> 01:03:47.330
So what happened was you took that turn and
meanwhile that underground river was flowing

01:03:47.330 --> 01:03:51.240
through you in a negative way, went into a
negative channel.

01:03:51.240 --> 01:03:52.240
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:03:52.240 --> 01:03:53.240
Got diverted.

01:03:53.240 --> 01:03:54.240
Steven Pressfield: Because you weren't expressing
it.

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:56.849
And instead of giving you energy, it was draining
your energy.

01:03:56.849 --> 01:03:57.849
Right?

01:03:57.849 --> 01:03:58.849
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:03:58.849 --> 01:03:59.849
Steven Pressfield: And then you had a moment
where you said, "Well, what the fuck?

01:03:59.849 --> 01:04:01.039
Let me just do this thing."

01:04:01.039 --> 01:04:02.039
Right?

01:04:02.039 --> 01:04:03.039
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:04:03.039 --> 01:04:05.660
Steven Pressfield: And you started to do it
and all of a sudden the energy, the battery

01:04:05.660 --> 01:04:11.529
got plugged in, got plugged into the wall,
and you came out of it.

01:04:11.529 --> 01:04:23.380
So a passage to the wilderness, to me, starts
with a denial of some creative thing or moral

01:04:23.380 --> 01:04:27.970
or ethical or expression of love.

01:04:27.970 --> 01:04:35.869
That usually we deny it out of fear or because
our conditioning tells us, "This is foolish,

01:04:35.869 --> 01:04:38.619
this is self-indulgent," this is whatever.

01:04:38.619 --> 01:04:39.619
Right?

01:04:39.619 --> 01:04:40.619
So "I can't do that.

01:04:40.619 --> 01:04:41.619
I can't do that.

01:04:41.619 --> 01:04:46.349
I can't sail across the Atlantic Ocean thinking
I'm going to get to the East Indies.

01:04:46.349 --> 01:04:48.050
That's insanity, right?"

01:04:48.050 --> 01:04:51.779
So we don't do it, but we pay the price.

01:04:51.779 --> 01:04:57.680
And that price is our time in the wilderness
until finally it gets so bad that we have

01:04:57.680 --> 01:05:00.309
to say, "Where did I fall off the track?"

01:05:00.309 --> 01:05:05.430
Or somehow we get back to that point and we
finally do embrace the thing that we were

01:05:05.430 --> 01:05:09.880
afraid of, and then we're okay, and then we're
out of it, I think.

01:05:09.880 --> 01:05:10.980
That's my theory.

01:05:10.980 --> 01:05:14.349
Tim Ferriss: I like the theory.

01:05:14.349 --> 01:05:18.470
I mean, it does make sense to me with so many
things.

01:05:18.470 --> 01:05:25.260
If you suppress them and attempt to compartmentalize
them or block them, divert them, they squeeze

01:05:25.260 --> 01:05:30.520
out in where they can squeeze out the edges
in very pathological ways.

01:05:30.520 --> 01:05:33.130
Because you're just —
Steven Pressfield: They come out in addiction.

01:05:33.130 --> 01:05:39.089
They come out in depression and alienation,
and all that sort of stuff.

01:05:39.089 --> 01:05:40.089
I think.

01:05:40.089 --> 01:05:41.310
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I agree with you.

01:05:41.310 --> 01:05:45.350
In our last conversation you mentioned Richard
Rohr.

01:05:45.350 --> 01:05:46.380
Steven Pressfield: Yeah.

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:51.150
Tim Ferriss: Franciscan Monk, I want to say?

01:05:51.150 --> 01:05:52.150
Steven Pressfield: Yeah.

01:05:52.150 --> 01:05:54.420
Tim Ferriss: So Richard believed the first
half of your life is creating the vessel that

01:05:54.420 --> 01:05:55.420
is your life.

01:05:55.420 --> 01:05:57.339
And the second half is filling the vessel.

01:05:57.339 --> 01:06:01.200
Is that something that you still find resonates
with you?

01:06:01.200 --> 01:06:02.700
Steven Pressfield: Absolutely.

01:06:02.700 --> 01:06:06.410
Richard Rohr is a Franciscan or Benedictine
monk.

01:06:06.410 --> 01:06:09.720
I think he lives in Albuquerque or somewhere
like that.

01:06:09.720 --> 01:06:11.990
And he wrote a book called Falling Upward.

01:06:11.990 --> 01:06:13.490
This is what we were talking about.

01:06:13.490 --> 01:06:14.490
Tim Ferriss: This is third or fourth time
this book has come up.

01:06:14.490 --> 01:06:15.490
Steven Pressfield: And Diana turned me on
to.

01:06:15.490 --> 01:06:17.030
Tim Ferriss: This is the third or fourth time
this book has come up in the last probably

01:06:17.030 --> 01:06:18.030
month for me.

01:06:18.030 --> 01:06:20.400
So I feel like I should probably take a look
at it.

01:06:20.400 --> 01:06:22.880
Steven Pressfield: Yeah, it's short.

01:06:22.880 --> 01:06:29.329
And basically his concept, like you just said,
is that the first half of our life is about

01:06:29.329 --> 01:06:33.520
establishing our ego identity.

01:06:33.520 --> 01:06:35.140
"I have a job.

01:06:35.140 --> 01:06:36.360
I have a wife and kids.

01:06:36.360 --> 01:06:37.530
I have a house.

01:06:37.530 --> 01:06:38.530
I'm a lawyer."

01:06:38.530 --> 01:06:39.859
Blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

01:06:39.859 --> 01:06:45.600
And you talk about your friends that had reached
the age of 40 when you were on the hike, right?

01:06:45.600 --> 01:06:49.330
At that point, people start to think, "Well,
okay, I built up this thing.

01:06:49.330 --> 01:06:50.330
Why?

01:06:50.330 --> 01:06:51.560
What am I going to do with it?"

01:06:51.560 --> 01:06:54.579
It's a vessel according to Richard Rohr.

01:06:54.579 --> 01:06:57.319
And what am I now going to fill that vessel
with?

01:06:57.319 --> 01:07:00.630
And I think it's a fundamental shift.

01:07:00.630 --> 01:07:07.240
The second half of life now becomes usually
more about giving to other people or about

01:07:07.240 --> 01:07:13.510
really finding what our real creative center
is and going for that.

01:07:13.510 --> 01:07:19.660
Now, I wrote a book called The Artist's Journey
that — I had never heard of Richard Rohr.

01:07:19.660 --> 01:07:23.720
I saw this as a completely same concept and
in a slightly different way.

01:07:23.720 --> 01:07:30.220
And what I said was that the first half of
our life is our hero's journey, which is sort

01:07:30.220 --> 01:07:38.480
of like Odysseus, our time in the wilderness,
whatever that ends when we sort find our calling.

01:07:38.480 --> 01:07:43.440
We say, "Okay, I'm a dancer," or, "Okay, I'm
an environmental activist," or whatever.

01:07:43.440 --> 01:07:47.200
And we say, "I don't give a shit what happens
to me.

01:07:47.200 --> 01:07:48.441
This is what I am I'm going to do.

01:07:48.441 --> 01:07:49.680
I'm going to do it."

01:07:49.680 --> 01:07:55.670
And then the second half, I would say, rather
than our hero's journey, is our artist's journey.

01:07:55.670 --> 01:08:02.890
At that point, we actually start to produce
the works that we've been running away from

01:08:02.890 --> 01:08:06.700
producing for all that time.

01:08:06.700 --> 01:08:13.470
If you're Bruce Springsteen, you suddenly
start doing album, album, album, album, album,

01:08:13.470 --> 01:08:16.199
and your life changes at that point.

01:08:16.199 --> 01:08:23.600
I think, in outer sense, it becomes much more
boring because you're working on your shit,

01:08:23.600 --> 01:08:24.600
right?

01:08:24.600 --> 01:08:25.600
You're producing the work.

01:08:25.600 --> 01:08:31.179
If you're Twyla Tharp, you're going to your
dance studio every morning and you're doing

01:08:31.179 --> 01:08:33.489
choreography, whatever it is that you're doing.

01:08:33.489 --> 01:08:34.489
Right?

01:08:34.489 --> 01:08:38.920
Or if you're Stephen King, you're writing
books.

01:08:38.920 --> 01:08:43.440
So I would agree with Richard Rohr that life
is divided in half.

01:08:43.440 --> 01:08:47.880
The first half you start searching for your
real authentic self.

01:08:47.880 --> 01:08:54.180
And once you find it and you become attached
to your gift, the second half is, "How do

01:08:54.180 --> 01:08:56.159
I deliver that gift?

01:08:56.159 --> 01:09:01.790
And how do I train myself so that I'm capable
of handling the voltage?"

01:09:01.790 --> 01:09:04.290
And it's sort of what you were saying, Tim,
about —

01:09:04.290 --> 01:09:06.041
Tim Ferriss: Actually, I want to underline
that, handling the voltage.

01:09:06.041 --> 01:09:08.480
So please don't lose your place, but that's
an interesting line.

01:09:08.480 --> 01:09:10.390
Can you say more about that?

01:09:10.390 --> 01:09:12.390
Steven Pressfield: Yeah.

01:09:12.390 --> 01:09:19.199
But also before I say that, yes, you're also
learning the craft.

01:09:19.199 --> 01:09:27.400
Let's say your calling is to produce the fastest
land-driven vehicle in the world, motorcycle,

01:09:27.400 --> 01:09:29.640
car, whatever it is.

01:09:29.640 --> 01:09:31.929
Now you've got to learn that skill.

01:09:31.929 --> 01:09:36.760
You probably already have it, but you've got
to learn it, so the car won't disintegrate

01:09:36.760 --> 01:09:40.750
on Lake Bonneville at 600 miles an hour.

01:09:40.750 --> 01:09:48.429
But back to handling the voltage, it's like
when you are now sitting down to right, and

01:09:48.429 --> 01:09:53.620
you're tapping into that whatever that creative
flow is of the eight different worlds and

01:09:53.620 --> 01:10:00.250
all that sort of stuff, that's voltage coming
from another dimension of reality or coming

01:10:00.250 --> 01:10:04.090
from inside your soul or wherever it's coming
from.

01:10:04.090 --> 01:10:11.640
And that voltage can overwhelm us if we're
not ready to do it.

01:10:11.640 --> 01:10:15.900
I know I have a friend, I'm sure we all have
friends that are deeply into meditation, and

01:10:15.900 --> 01:10:23.060
they say that you really have to be physically
fit in addition to being spiritually and psychologically

01:10:23.060 --> 01:10:27.890
fit once you get into the deeper levels of
meditation because whatever energy is coming

01:10:27.890 --> 01:10:32.449
down — I don't know this myself, I take
this second hand, is coming and it can be

01:10:32.449 --> 01:10:33.449
too much.

01:10:33.449 --> 01:10:34.449
You can't handle it.

01:10:34.449 --> 01:10:35.980
It's like psychedelics, right?

01:10:35.980 --> 01:10:37.860
It'll blow your mind, right?

01:10:37.860 --> 01:10:42.190
You have to have some sort of a capacity to
endure that.

01:10:42.190 --> 01:10:43.650
Tim Ferriss: You have to build and cultivate
capacity.

01:10:43.650 --> 01:10:47.690
Steven Pressfield: So we do have to sort of
learn, in the second half of our lives, I

01:10:47.690 --> 01:10:49.620
think, to handle that voltage.

01:10:49.620 --> 01:10:55.020
Tim Ferriss: What did it feel like to you
to write a memoir?

01:10:55.020 --> 01:10:59.810
Because you mentioned some of your hesitation
around whether or not it would be interesting

01:10:59.810 --> 01:11:03.010
to other people, but it was interesting to
me, interesting to Rich Roll.

01:11:03.010 --> 01:11:07.970
Interesting also, goes without saying, to
millions of people who heard those conversations.

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:11.950
And then to your significant other.

01:11:11.950 --> 01:11:17.541
What was it like then to sit down and write
a memoir, whether at the beginning or in the

01:11:17.541 --> 01:11:18.541
middle?

01:11:18.541 --> 01:11:19.541
Steven Pressfield: It's a great question,
Tim.

01:11:19.541 --> 01:11:24.910
It was really challenging in the sense that
the resistance that you feel, I think to writing

01:11:24.910 --> 01:11:31.139
a memoir, the voice in your head says, "Who's
going to give a fuck about your stupid stories?

01:11:31.139 --> 01:11:33.060
Everybody in the world's got a million of
these things.

01:11:33.060 --> 01:11:34.969
Why is yours going to be any different?"

01:11:34.969 --> 01:11:37.660
So that was a big part of it.

01:11:37.660 --> 01:11:42.570
The flip side of that was, "Before I die,
I want to put some of this shit down.

01:11:42.570 --> 01:11:44.960
It shouldn't just go away."

01:11:44.960 --> 01:11:50.939
Even if it's only my family, my nephews and
nieces, that they should be able to read this.

01:11:50.939 --> 01:11:55.489
But then above and beyond that, and this is,
I know where we've been trending into this

01:11:55.489 --> 01:12:01.190
conversation about the hardcore physical reality
of this stuff was like, "How do I tell this

01:12:01.190 --> 01:12:02.190
fucking story?

01:12:02.190 --> 01:12:04.070
What do I leave out?

01:12:04.070 --> 01:12:05.870
What do I leave in?

01:12:05.870 --> 01:12:07.440
What's the point of view?

01:12:07.440 --> 01:12:08.910
What's my tone of voice?

01:12:08.910 --> 01:12:12.600
Who is I in this story, if I'm telling it
in the first person?"

01:12:12.600 --> 01:12:14.139
So that was sort of a big challenge.

01:12:14.139 --> 01:12:19.350
And again, I sort did what we were talking
about before, or what I was talking about,

01:12:19.350 --> 01:12:26.679
Blitzkrieg of it would've overwhelmed me if
I didn't just do it.

01:12:26.679 --> 01:12:29.219
So I just did it.

01:12:29.219 --> 01:12:32.050
When it was done, I'd left out a lot of chunks
and a lot of this.

01:12:32.050 --> 01:12:38.210
I thought, I wished I had a real editor, which
I didn't at this point.

01:12:38.210 --> 01:12:42.289
And I just sort had to make the decision of,
"I'm going to edit it myself.

01:12:42.289 --> 01:12:43.289
This is it.

01:12:43.289 --> 01:12:46.870
This is what it's going to be for good or
ill."

01:12:46.870 --> 01:12:53.770
Tim Ferriss: So Govt Cheese, what's the title
about?

01:12:53.770 --> 01:13:01.530
Steven Pressfield: One of the articles that
we delivered in this trucking company was

01:13:01.530 --> 01:13:07.510
surplus food to poor communities on the coast
in North Carolina.

01:13:07.510 --> 01:13:14.679
And because I was the most junior driver for
this company, this was the lowest-paying job

01:13:14.679 --> 01:13:21.370
and the load that people wanted the least.

01:13:21.370 --> 01:13:23.520
To make these runs was like, I would make
$15.

01:13:23.520 --> 01:13:24.659
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

01:13:24.659 --> 01:13:29.690
Steven Pressfield: But I really loved these
runs.

01:13:29.690 --> 01:13:35.550
And you would make them — you would drive
all night and arrive really early before dawn.

01:13:35.550 --> 01:13:37.739
It was always to a church.

01:13:37.739 --> 01:13:44.010
You always distributed at a church and it
was always a Black church with a Black congregation.

01:13:44.010 --> 01:13:47.900
A lot of the recipients were sharecroppers
and stuff like that.

01:13:47.900 --> 01:13:53.000
But they also were, sometimes you'd have guys
come in with hot Mustangs, with great-looking

01:13:53.000 --> 01:13:56.270
young girls, that kind of stuff.

01:13:56.270 --> 01:14:05.490
What you were delivering, the government cheese
and the dried powdered milk and pinto beans

01:14:05.490 --> 01:14:08.900
and stuff like that, was stuff that was going
on people's tables.

01:14:08.900 --> 01:14:11.130
It was going to keep them alive.

01:14:11.130 --> 01:14:16.239
So you really felt like you were doing some
good.

01:14:16.239 --> 01:14:19.740
I just really enjoyed these things.

01:14:19.740 --> 01:14:22.090
I can't tell you how much fun they were.

01:14:22.090 --> 01:14:27.790
And the other aspect is you were anonymous
in these things.

01:14:27.790 --> 01:14:32.380
When you would pull into the lot, I never
had anybody — you would talk to the minister.

01:14:32.380 --> 01:14:35.659
The minister would tell you, "Pull your vehicle
over here," da da, da, da.

01:14:35.659 --> 01:14:38.100
And they would always address you as "Driver."

01:14:38.100 --> 01:14:41.540
They'd say, "Driver, would you mind pulling
this over here?"

01:14:41.540 --> 01:14:42.570
Never ask you your name.

01:14:42.570 --> 01:14:43.570
I don't know why.

01:14:43.570 --> 01:14:46.830
And your role was really to recede into the
background.

01:14:46.830 --> 01:14:47.889
You didn't participate.

01:14:47.889 --> 01:14:52.000
You opened the doors, and they took the food
off the way they wanted to.

01:14:52.000 --> 01:14:56.500
So when all was set and done, and this kind
of goes back to the story I was telling about

01:14:56.500 --> 01:15:04.770
Hugh Reeves, I feel like writing is about
delivering a load and its sustenance.

01:15:04.770 --> 01:15:06.659
You hope it's sustenance, right?

01:15:06.659 --> 01:15:13.469
It's a load of surplus food, of government
cheese, that's going to go on people's tables.

01:15:13.469 --> 01:15:17.980
And the other aspect about that is you are
a vehicle for it.

01:15:17.980 --> 01:15:21.719
I didn't grow the cheese.

01:15:21.719 --> 01:15:23.210
All I did was deliver it.

01:15:23.210 --> 01:15:25.639
When I'm done, the trailer is empty.

01:15:25.639 --> 01:15:29.870
I close it up and I go away and do it again
somewhere else.

01:15:29.870 --> 01:15:32.940
It's a metaphor for writing for me in other
words.

01:15:32.940 --> 01:15:33.940
That's what it was.

01:15:33.940 --> 01:15:35.130
Tim Ferriss: That is a great backstory.

01:15:35.130 --> 01:15:37.260
Steven Pressfield: That's what "government
cheese" means.

01:15:37.260 --> 01:15:41.980
Tim Ferriss: What else would you like people
to know about this memoir?

01:15:41.980 --> 01:15:44.890
Is there anything else that you would like
to say about it?

01:15:44.890 --> 01:15:46.449
I mean, your stories are incredible.

01:15:46.449 --> 01:15:48.130
Your writing is incredible.

01:15:48.130 --> 01:15:54.160
The cover quotes you have are spectacular
from a lot of people I respect.

01:15:54.160 --> 01:15:58.650
What else would you like to say about Govt
Cheese, if anything?

01:15:58.650 --> 01:16:02.260
Steven Pressfield: There is one other thing
I would like to say.

01:16:02.260 --> 01:16:05.820
Like I say, it's a writer's odyssey.

01:16:05.820 --> 01:16:09.719
It goes from somebody that at the start, can't
do it.

01:16:09.719 --> 01:16:15.730
At the end of it, can do it.

01:16:15.730 --> 01:16:22.699
One of the big takeaways from my story at
least is that there are a series of breakthroughs

01:16:22.699 --> 01:16:29.260
along the way, emotional, but almost always,
they never pay off in the moment.

01:16:29.260 --> 01:16:33.870
It's like you have a breakthrough and nothing
happens.

01:16:33.870 --> 01:16:38.120
It's like 10 years later, it pays off.

01:16:38.120 --> 01:16:46.739
So that if anybody is listening to us today,
Tim, and trying to get any sustenance out

01:16:46.739 --> 01:16:50.880
of that, one of the big lessons is it takes
a long time.

01:16:50.880 --> 01:16:51.980
It's a real process.

01:16:51.980 --> 01:16:55.090
You're working on your eight houses thing.

01:16:55.090 --> 01:16:59.520
It may be a few years before you really get
a handle on this thing.

01:16:59.520 --> 01:17:00.520
Tim Ferriss: For sure.

01:17:00.520 --> 01:17:05.540
Steven Pressfield: It would be amazing if
you came right out of the chute and it worked.

01:17:05.540 --> 01:17:06.610
Yeah.

01:17:06.610 --> 01:17:13.620
It seems to me that the way these breakthroughs
work is they need to mature, that they take

01:17:13.620 --> 01:17:14.620
time.

01:17:14.620 --> 01:17:17.139
You think, "Oh, I finished a book."

01:17:17.139 --> 01:17:20.000
And then for me, I couldn't sell that book.

01:17:20.000 --> 01:17:21.889
I couldn't sell the next book.

01:17:21.889 --> 01:17:25.890
It was years and years before I could.

01:17:25.890 --> 01:17:32.700
But there is a process and there is significance
and change is happening.

01:17:32.700 --> 01:17:37.280
Tim Ferriss: Steven Pressfield, what a story
you have.

01:17:37.280 --> 01:17:38.300
What a life you've had, too.

01:17:38.300 --> 01:17:39.310
And what a life you will have.

01:17:39.310 --> 01:17:40.880
Steven Pressfield: What a life you have.

01:17:40.880 --> 01:17:42.989
And so does everybody that's listening to
us.

01:17:42.989 --> 01:17:49.139
Tim Ferriss: Before we wind this second conversation
to a close, is there anything else that you

01:17:49.139 --> 01:17:50.270
would like to add?

01:17:50.270 --> 01:17:51.870
Anything else you'd like to point people to?

01:17:51.870 --> 01:17:54.679
Of course, you have many books.

01:17:54.679 --> 01:18:00.060
The newest book is your memoir, Govt Cheese,
which is about all those years before the

01:18:00.060 --> 01:18:04.940
first publication, which in a way, I don't
want to say is the most important, but to

01:18:04.940 --> 01:18:12.940
me is certainly one of the most interesting
untold stories, because it sets the foundation.

01:18:12.940 --> 01:18:17.160
It talks about the breakthroughs that took
a long time to germinate it.

01:18:17.160 --> 01:18:25.889
It explains all of the development in the
form of these stories and these mentors leading

01:18:25.889 --> 01:18:32.420
up to you becoming the Steven Pressfield that
people recognize today.

01:18:32.420 --> 01:18:34.489
Is there anything else that you would like
to add?

01:18:34.489 --> 01:18:37.730
Any other closing comments or things you'd
like to point people to?

01:18:37.730 --> 01:18:41.430
Of course, you've got the Instagram video
series and the wilderness.

01:18:41.430 --> 01:18:45.489
You've got the website, Steven Pressfield,
again, folks with a V. And then people can

01:18:45.489 --> 01:18:50.679
find you on Twitter @SPressfield and on other
social that we will link to in the show notes.

01:18:50.679 --> 01:18:56.800
But anything else that you'd like to add or
make any requests of my audience?

01:18:56.800 --> 01:18:57.800
Anything at all?

01:18:57.800 --> 01:19:02.620
Steven Pressfield: I would just say to you,
Tim, for your story that you're working on

01:19:02.620 --> 01:19:11.271
now, for whatever this is, that to trust in
that greater wisdom, whatever that is, some

01:19:11.271 --> 01:19:14.969
goddess somewhere knows what she's doing.

01:19:14.969 --> 01:19:16.150
Tim Ferriss: I like that.

01:19:16.150 --> 01:19:18.350
Steven Pressfield: You may not know what you're
doing.

01:19:18.350 --> 01:19:20.320
Tim Ferriss: Take some of the weight off my
shoulders.

01:19:20.320 --> 01:19:23.500
Steven Pressfield: But I think it's great
that you're trusting in it.

01:19:23.500 --> 01:19:27.159
Even though it's absurd, even though it may
totally fall on its face, which I'm sure it

01:19:27.159 --> 01:19:28.159
won't.

01:19:28.159 --> 01:19:29.430
So that's what I would say to you.

01:19:29.430 --> 01:19:36.559
And really to anybody that's listening to
us that it always seems absurd at the start,

01:19:36.559 --> 01:19:40.130
just like Christopher Columbus going the wrong
way to the Indies.

01:19:40.130 --> 01:19:42.860
But I think that's our real life.

01:19:42.860 --> 01:19:46.060
All this other stuff is, it's okay.

01:19:46.060 --> 01:19:50.550
But that's our real life, I think.

01:19:50.550 --> 01:19:53.230
Belief is a big thing.

01:19:53.230 --> 01:19:58.500
We started out, the first thing was like,
"Do you have any beliefs that go contrary

01:19:58.500 --> 01:20:01.469
to dark reality or depression?"

01:20:01.469 --> 01:20:07.949
And I think the belief in this greater wisdom,
this other dimension of reality, that's the

01:20:07.949 --> 01:20:08.949
key thing.

01:20:08.949 --> 01:20:11.150
And it's so hard to do.

01:20:11.150 --> 01:20:16.080
Again, you just have to deny all the other
— this doesn't count.

01:20:16.080 --> 01:20:18.350
I don't believe those voices.

01:20:18.350 --> 01:20:20.520
These voices, I dismiss them too.

01:20:20.520 --> 01:20:24.760
I'm just going to stay in this lane and just
keep going.

01:20:24.760 --> 01:20:30.170
So that's why I say to you, Tim, and everybody
that's listening to us and to myself, same

01:20:30.170 --> 01:20:32.840
thing,
Tim Ferriss: Steven, so nice to actually spend

01:20:32.840 --> 01:20:34.170
some time in person.

01:20:34.170 --> 01:20:35.170
Steven Pressfield: Tim, it's been great.

01:20:35.170 --> 01:20:36.170
Tim Ferriss: So lovely.

01:20:36.170 --> 01:20:41.030
And to everybody listening and watching, we'll
link to everything, including the new book,

01:20:41.030 --> 01:20:44.180
Govt Cheese, by Mr. Steven Pressfield.

01:20:44.180 --> 01:20:54.050
We'll also link to any resources, any gods,
any myths that we may have mentioned at tim.blog/podcast

01:20:54.050 --> 01:20:55.050
as per usual.

01:20:55.050 --> 01:20:59.820
Until next time, just be a little bit kinder
than is necessary to yourself as well.

01:20:59.820 --> 01:21:04.929
Believe in that greater wisdom, pick a lane
that matters to you.

01:21:04.929 --> 01:21:06.040
And thanks for tuning in.

01:21:06.040 --> 01:21:07.820
Steven Pressfield: All right.

01:21:07.820 --> 01:21:10.489
[NEWS CLIP]
Tim Ferriss: Thank you so much for listening,

01:21:10.489 --> 01:21:11.489
everybody.

01:21:11.489 --> 01:21:13.870
Thank you also to Steve for coming on the
show.

01:21:13.870 --> 01:21:18.540
Some of the fiction writing discussed in our
interview is now live on a brand-new podcast,

01:21:18.540 --> 01:21:23.719
called COCKPUNCH—as it sounds, that is how
it’s spelled, believe it or not.

01:21:23.719 --> 01:21:28.050
It launched last week with a short and very
bizarre, let’s call it, movie trailer, and

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:32.510
the podcast debuted at number one in the fiction
category on Apple Podcasts.

01:21:32.510 --> 01:21:37.820
It’s now been in the top 100 for all podcasts
on Apple Podcasts in the US and other countries

01:21:37.820 --> 01:21:38.820
all week.

01:21:38.820 --> 01:21:42.220
This is the first new podcast I’ve launched
in many years.

01:21:42.220 --> 01:21:45.980
The episodes are very short, roughly five
minutes each, so please check them out.

01:21:45.980 --> 01:21:49.270
You can find them anywhere—Apple Podcasts,
Spotify, wherever you listen to your fine

01:21:49.270 --> 01:21:54.810
podcasts—and this entire thing is intended
to add some laughter and levity to a world

01:21:54.810 --> 01:21:57.400
dominated by doomscrolling and pessimism.

01:21:57.400 --> 01:22:01.010
So if you like fantasy, if you’re looking
for a little humor, and you want to see me

01:22:01.010 --> 01:22:04.900
take a stab at fiction, check it out.

01:22:04.900 --> 01:22:05.560
Cockpunch.com/podcast.

